\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Workers Are Getting Fired Over Posts Mocking Kirk's Death\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison Pohle and Chip Cutter \par \uc2 979 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers across the country who have mocked Charlie Kirk's death online have quickly learned their words can get them fired.\par \par \uc2 From \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  to Nasdaq -- and in workplaces that include restaurants, schools and law firms -- employers have ousted or suspended staff in recent days for gloating, deriding or making otherwise contentious posts about the conservative activist's killing. Many are getting flagged to the posts by online activists who have collected the names of commenters. Some prominent conservatives have joined the call, too.\par \par \uc2 "When you see someone celebrating Charlie's murder, call them out -- and, hell, call their employer," said Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  as he guest-hosted the late Kirk's podcast Monday.\par \par \uc2 The swift response from companies underscores their eagerness to distance themselves from political commentary that risks blowing back on their reputations with consumers -- or the Trump administration. Business leaders have become less accommodating in recent years to their workers' personal views on matters including the war in Gaza, immigration and transgender rights.\par \par \uc2 The outcry over employees' comments on Kirk, though, has posed a new challenge for employers, executives say.\par \par \uc2 "This is very different," said Vanessa Matsis-McCready, associate general counsel and vice president of HR services for Engage PEO, which handles workplace functions for small and medium businesses. "I really don't feel like there was a time when you could have an individual make a statement on their personal social media and have their employer receive thousands of phone calls."\par \par \uc2 Employers including Washington law firm \uc2 Perkins Coie\uc2 , the \uc2 Carolina Panthers\uc2  and others have said they fired employees who made comments or posted online in ways that didn't match their values.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service put an agent on immediate leave for a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post that mentioned "karma." \uc2 ODP Corporation\uc2 , the parent company of \uc2 Office Depot\uc2 , said it fired workers after a video appeared to show an employee refusing to print a poster for a Kirk vigil.\par \par \uc2 One anonymously run website had crowdsourced tens of thousands of commenters' names, social-media accounts and employers. Some posts cheered his death, while others disparaged or mocked some of Kirk's more polarizing views. Another conservative political activist, Olivia Krolczyk, posted on X over the weekend that she had contacted 471 employers.\par \par \uc2 Companies have quickly responded, though some people say their comments have been misconstrued. On Friday, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  tagged \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Satya Nadella\uc2  as he reposted what another X user called a list of developers at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's game developer Blizzard "trashing" Kirk. "What's going on here?" Musk wrote in the post to his 226 million-plus followers.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  released a statement, saying, "We're aware of the views expressed by a small subset of our employees regarding recent events." It added that the company was "currently reviewing each individual situation." Musk didn't respond to requests for comment Monday.\par \par \uc2 Some Trump cabinet members have urged the disciplinary actions. Speaking to \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  on Fox News late Monday, Attorney General Pam Bondi said employers had an obligation to get rid of such workers.\par \par \uc2 "You need to look at people who are saying horrible things. And they shouldn't be working with you," she said. She added that Justice Department officials would look into the \uc2 Office Depot\uc2  incident. "Businesses cannot discriminate," she said. "We can prosecute you for that."\par \par \uc2 Transportation Secretary \uc2 Sean Duffy\uc2  endorsed \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 ' decision to remove employees from service for posts related to Kirk's death. "This behavior is disgusting and they should be fired," he posted on X. "Any company responsible for the safety of the traveling public cannot tolerate that behavior."\par \par \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  and \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  also have pulled employees from duties over Kirk-related posts. "We've been clear with our customers and employees that there's zero tolerance for politically motivated violence or any attempt to justify it," United said. In a memo to Delta employees, CEO \uc2 Ed Bastian\uc2  said the posts of suspended Delta employees "went well beyond healthy, respectful debate."\par \par \uc2 For workers, the quick firings are the latest reminder that business leaders have waning tolerance for disruptive political speech. Private-sector employees aren't legally protected from repercussions for words or actions even if it happens outside the workplace, legal experts say.\par \par \uc2 Many employers have "reached a tipping point," said Dawn Solowey, a partner at law firm \uc2 Seyfarth Shaw\uc2 , who noted that corporate bosses now want to "lower the temperature."\par \par \uc2 One challenge for employers now is deciding which posts cross a line and should result in an employee's termination, human-resources specialists say. In some cases, workers say they lost their jobs for posts that didn't endorse violence or celebrate Kirk's murder.\par \par \uc2 The online campaigns also have wrongly implicated some people. Cynthia Rehberg, an associate principal from a Wisconsin elementary school, found her staff photo had been inaccurately posted on X along with a social-media post celebrating Kirk's death. The school district said it received thousands of comments, along with hundreds of phone calls. Some made direct threats to Rehberg, who says she has nothing but sympathy for the Kirk family.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Fournier, a conservative activist, had promoted the post and took it down late Sunday, several days after the rumor began circulating, she said.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, about 100 students called out of school due to safety concerns, the superintendent, Jason Tadlock, said.\par \par \uc2 Fournier later clarified on X that Rehberg hadn't posted the Kirk-related comment.\par \par \uc2 Rehberg said she hopes the episode is a reminder that not every claim made on social media is accurate and that real people are affected by such public accusations.\par \par \uc2 "It's just a good lesson for students, adults, all, where, when we come across a post, that we look at it critically."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250917el9h00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
