\par\par \b \uc2 World News: Seoul Probes Raid in U.S. --- South Korea will investigate workers' treatment after arrests in Georgia\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jiyoung Sohn and Christopher Otts \par \uc2 474 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SEOUL -- South Korea is conducting a government investigation into potential human-rights violations against more than 300 Korean citizens related to the U.S. immigration raid at a \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  construction site in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 The raid this month has sparked anger across South Korea, which received no advanced notice and has pledged nearly half a trillion dollars in U.S. investments and energy purchases as part of its trade pact with Washington.\par \par \uc2 The outrage rose a notch following the repatriation of 316 South Koreans on Friday. Some of the Koreans, who had been shackled during the initial Sept. 4 raid, described alleged mistreatment, from moldy beds to the absence of on-hand interpreters to being held in 70-person temporary detention cells. One worker told local media he hadn't been read his Miranda rights.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's Foreign Ministry\uc2 , in coordination with the country's Justice Ministry and the former detainees' employers, is looking into whether any human-rights violations were committed inside the detention center.\par \par \uc2 They also will investigate whether any Korean was unfairly arrested based on the nature of their work.\par \par \uc2 If the investigation reveals violations, South Korea will file a formal complaint to the U.S. The probe is expected to wrap up in weeks, a South Korean official said.\par \par \uc2 During a visit to Seoul on Sunday, Christopher Landau, the U.S.'s deputy secretary of state, expressed deep regret over the Koreans' detainment.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 60% of South Koreans viewed the raid as excessive and expressed disappointment with the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , a local poll shows.\par \par \uc2 The raid -- in which roughly 475 workers were arrested -- occurred at an electric-vehicle battery site outside Savannah, Ga., jointly operated by Hyundai and fellow Korean firm \uc2 LG Energy Solution\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LG\uc2 , the third-largest electric-vehicle-battery manufacturer in the world, said it hasn't stopped construction of its plants in the U.S. despite the raid. \uc2 LG\uc2  has four battery plants under construction in the U.S., including joint ventures with \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "All of the locations are working very hard right now to assess and to make sure that we don't have an impact on our customers," said Bob Lee, the battery maker's top executive in North America. Lee was speaking to reporters on the sidelines of an automotive conference in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 It was the largest single-site immigration raid ever conducted by U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement.\par \par \uc2 The initial targets of the Hyundai raid were four Hispanic workers, according to the search warrant.\par \par \uc2 South Korea is one of the U.S.'s largest sources of foreign direct investment, often in strategic industries that lack homegrown American alternatives, such as EV batteries.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250917el9h00005\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
