\par\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2 's AI Recruits, Old Guard At Odds\b0\par\par\uc2 By Meghan Bobrowsky, Keach Hagey and Berber Jin \par \uc2 811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  spent millions of dollars this summer recruiting AI stars. Now comes the hard part of making them work with their new colleagues.\par \par \uc2 Some of \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 's expensive new recruits have already defected to other AI labs. Existing employees have jockeyed for new spots within \uc2 Meta\uc2 's restructured AI organization or lobbied for raises in light of the influx of highly paid new colleagues. At least one who was given a grant worth millions of dollars left anyway, saying they believed newcomers were still making multiples more.\par \par \uc2 The pay packages may be massive, and the company might be worth nearly $2 trillion. But at its core, \uc2 Meta\uc2  is facing a quintessential management problem: how to recruit and retain top-tier talent while keeping remaining employees satisfied, and maintaining harmony across the organization.\par \par \uc2 "If you don't lay the groundwork culturally for bringing in these stars, you're going to end up burning a bunch of them out and pissing them off, and a bunch of them are going to quit and you're going to waste millions of dollars," said \uc2 Laszlo Bock\uc2 , a tech industry adviser and former head of people operations at \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The new AI group's most elite squad of AI researchers -- members of the TBD Lab -- sit in an area of the company's Menlo Park, Calif., headquarters near Zuckerberg's desk that requires special badge access for entry, according to people familiar with the matter. Their work is closely guarded, and the names of its members aren't visible on the company's internal organization chart. Other names in the organization are viewable.\par \par \uc2 Some employees pointed to the measures as reflecting new status distinctions within the company at a time teams are competing for computer resources and a hiring freeze has made it difficult to fill roles without personal approval from \uc2 Meta\uc2  Chief AI Officer \uc2 Alexandr Wang\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a written statement, a \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesman called reporting on the company's AI operation "navel-gazing" and said, "This is yet another series of false, exaggerated or mischaracterized claims."\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  isn't the only AI player dealing with this issue. The all-out war for talent has forced companies large and small to take extraordinary measures to keep their employees happy.\par \par \uc2 In August, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  gave some of its research and engineering staff a one-time bonus, with some employees receiving millions of dollars, according to people familiar with the matter. \uc2 Apple\uc2  this summer lost a string of AI employees, including one of the leaders developing its AI models, to rival labs.\par \par \uc2 And staffers at \uc2 Safe Superintelligence\uc2 , a startup co-founded by \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  co-founder Ilya Sutskever, are discouraged from mentioning the company on their \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  profiles, in part to prevent other companies from trying to poach them.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  has succeeded in recruiting more than 50 people with Zuckerberg's promise of abundant computing resources, alongside rich compensation. Of those, at least 21 people are from \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , more than a dozen came from \uc2 Google\uc2 , a handful came from \uc2 xAI\uc2  and others from \uc2 Apple\uc2 , according to people familiar with the matter and public \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  profiles.\par \par \uc2 Some \uc2 Meta\uc2  employees have had success leveraging competing offers to get a share of the money and prestige Zuckerberg has been lavishing on outside recruits. In July, a handful of employees from one of \uc2 Meta\uc2 's AI infrastructure teams secured offers from former \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  executive \uc2 Mira Murati\uc2 's new AI startup, \uc2 Thinking Machines Lab\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After the employees took the offers to \uc2 Meta\uc2 , their compensation was increased and they were moved to the TBD Lab team. A spokesman for \uc2 Meta\uc2  said the company had already been planning to move those staffers to the new team and adjust their compensation "regardless of any recruitment offers they may have received" and that the company hasn't made counteroffers to employees threatening to leave.\par \par \uc2 While the recruiting frenzy has abated somewhat since \uc2 Meta\uc2  imposed its hiring freeze, the intensely competitive talent market has encouraged researchers to be choosy and ping pong around AI labs with ease.\par \par \uc2 Rishabh Agarwal, a Toronto-based AI researcher who joined \uc2 Meta\uc2 's AI division in April, received an offer to join the superintelligence lab after asking Zuckerberg for a role, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 He decided to leave for a startup called Periodic Labs, founded by a former \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  executive, after being told he would have to work in-person at \uc2 Meta\uc2 's Menlo headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Two other new hires from \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 xAI\uc2 , Avi Verma and Ethan Knight, recently left for \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , according to people familiar with the matter and their public \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  profiles. Ruben Mayer, an executive who joined from \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  as a product manager, also left \uc2 Meta\uc2  for what he said were personal reasons. He hasn't announced a new job.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250911el9b0003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
