\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Offshore Wind Industry Stuck in Limbo\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Uberti and Jennifer Hiller \par \uc2 930 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Connor Walcutt is in a holding pattern that borders on the surreal.\par \par \uc2 Peering out from a 262-foot ship he currently calls home, the 26-year-old has been looking across the water off the coast of Rhode Island and onto one of America's largest construction sites, wondering if he will ever be allowed to finish the job.\par \par \uc2 Walcutt is among the workers caught in the crossfire of President Trump's war on the offshore wind industry, which recently culminated when officials halted the $5 billion Revolution Wind project. The administration's stop-work order surprised even the shellshocked clean-energy sector because the development's skyscraper-sized turbines are largely complete.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys general in Rhode Island and Connecticut sued the Trump administration to challenge what they called an unlawful move. So did the developers behind Revolution.\par \par \uc2 A small army of workers and suppliers on the East Coast and elsewhere are now racked by the possibility that a new industry for which they spent years preparing could soon disappear.\par \par \uc2 On the water but unable to work, Walcutt said about 20 specialists standing by on the vessel do their best to avoid the subject. "No one talks about it . . . That's when you start to worry," he said. "Tomorrow, I could be out of a job."\par \par \uc2 Twelve-hour shifts seven days a week have given way to restlessness on the ECO Edison. The first U.S.-made ship of its kind, the vessel was christened last year at a New Orleans event attended by U.S. House Majority Leader Steve Scalise (R., La.).\par \par \uc2 Meals on board are more subdued these days. Walcutt, who leads a group of site technicians who inspect the platforms that anchor turbines to the ocean floor, conducts safety drills with his team to stay sharp.\par \par \uc2 "When you're on standby, it's either eat, sleep or gym," Walcutt said.\par \par \uc2 The struggles that led offshore wind to this moment of crisis predate Trump. Former President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's climate law showered the sector with subsidies, but inflation sent costs skyrocketing. Governments up and down the East Coast competed for investment in ports and factories promising blue-collar jobs. Supply chains couldn't keep up. Local opposition busted project timelines. Some developers pulled out.\par \par \uc2 Although there is continuing development in federally overseen waters, investors fear Trump, who has long railed against offshore wind as an expensive and unreliable eyesore, could sound the death knell. The administration recently signaled in court filings that it intends to reconsider approvals for wind farms off the coasts of Maryland and Massachusetts. Advocates warn that plans in Virginia and elsewhere could be next.\par \par \uc2 The 65-turbine Revolution Wind is set to provide enough power for 350,000 homes in Rhode Island and Connecticut starting next year at less than 10 cents per kilowatt-hour for 20 years, well below the region's current market rate for electricity, although higher than current wholesale prices paid by utilities to generators. It has become the focal point of this fight because developers say it is 80% complete.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Ocean Energy Management\uc2  on Aug. 22 ordered the companies to stop work while officials "address concerns related to the protection of national security interests." In a television interview days later, Interior Secretary \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  cited potential interference with the radar technology needed to detect a "swarm drone attack," a threat security analysts say is overblown.\par \par \uc2 The agency's order drew a rebuke from the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2  and has enraged New England unions that have helped staff the construction project with an estimated 1,000 painters, electricians, pile drivers and more. A good number of those workers backed Trump last November, said Patrick Crowley, president of the Rhode Island \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "They didn't vote for this," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Rhode Island and Connecticut attorneys general are seeking to overturn what they called in a statement "arbitrary and capricious government conduct." Orsted and \uc2 Global Infrastructure Partners\uc2 , Revolution's developers, have leveled their own challenge to a move they said lacked legal authority. The Interior Department declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 "People's pocketbooks are going to be impacted in Rhode Island," Gov. \uc2 Dan McKee\uc2  said in an interview, adding that reneging on federally approved projects could chill new investment. "It also sends the wrong signal to businesses."\par \par \uc2 The region's power-grid operator, \uc2 ISO New England\uc2 , said in a statement that it is planning for the project to come online, and delays will increase risks to electric reliability.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Workers Wait, and Costs Pile Up\par \par \uc2 Union workers like Antonio Gianfrancesco are nervous and watching their phones for updates on the halted $5 billion Revolution Wind project off the coast of Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody is unsure of what to do because this was so abrupt," said the technician, who had been working on the ECO Edison.\par \par \uc2 Carpenter Tony Vaz, who was scheduled to return offshore to work in late August, went fishing one night recently to clear his head. Not far from Newport, he saw turbines from land for the first time.\par \par \uc2 "Please," the 41-year-old said, "just let us finish it."\par \par \uc2 Revolution's developers said in their complaint that the construction pause costs millions of dollars a week. About 20 vessels were at work on Revolution, whose supply chain includes 179 U.S. companies from 34 states, according to the Oceantic Network.\par \par \uc2 Revolution's developers said they would face more than $1 billion in breakaway costs if the project is canceled.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250909el990000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
