\par\par \b \uc2 Seoul Says U.S. Set to Release Korean Workers Held in Raid\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jiyoung Sohn, Timothy W. Martin and Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 1,040 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SEOUL -- South Korea and the U.S. have reached a deal to release Korean citizens who were detained last Thursday in a large-scale immigration raid at a \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  plant in Georgia, the office of the South Korean president said.\par \par \uc2 Some administrative procedures remain, but once they are cleared, a chartered plane will be sent to the U.S. to bring the South Korean citizens home, Kang Hoon-sik, chief of staff of South Korea's presidential office, said on Sunday. He didn't elaborate on the timing nor the nature of the deal with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The State Department doesn't comment on private diplomatic communications, a spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 One unresolved point is whether the detained South Koreans are allowed to leave voluntarily or be deported -- the latter move often triggering a multiyear entry ban to America, officials from Seoul and Washington said. As of Friday, the U.S. had said no criminal charges had been filed.\par \par \uc2 Seoul's statement of a deal comes days after U.S. law-enforcement officials carried out their largest-ever single-site immigration raid at the construction site of an electric-vehicle battery factory near Savannah, Ga. The factory is a joint venture between Hyundai and fellow South Korean firm \uc2 LG Energy Solution\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, U.S. border czar Tom Homan said on CNN that these workplace raids would continue. "You're going to see a lot more worksite enforcement operations," he said. "It's a crime to knowingly hire an illegal alien."\par \par \uc2 President Trump on Sunday evening said foreign companies' investments in the U.S. were welcome and they should "LEGALLY bring your very smart people, with great technical talent, to build World Class products, and we will make it quickly and legally possible for you to do so." In his Truth Social post, he added: "What we ask in return is that you hire and train American Workers."\par \par \uc2 Homan said employers hire people in the country illegally because they want to pay them less and undercut competing American workers.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of federal agents mustered just outside the sprawling \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  complex in Ellabell, Ga., that is called the "Metaplant." Behind the walls, workers assemble electric vehicles in a $7.6 billion site that is so new that some buildings are still under construction.\par \par \uc2 Immigration officers entered the property around 10:45 a.m. Thursday, and began separating a large group of workers by nationality and visa status. Groups were processed and then loaded onto buses. Some attempted to flee -- a handful ran into a sewage pond.\par \par \uc2 Of the 475 people who were detained in the raid, around 300 were South Korean citizens, \uc2 South Korea's foreign ministry\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 None of the detained were directly employed by Hyundai, the company said. LG Energy said 47 of its employees -- all South Koreans except one Indonesian -- had been arrested, as well as roughly 250 workers at subcontracted companies.\par \par \uc2 Kia Burke, a logistics manager at another part of the Hyundai site, said she wasn't surprised the facility was raided given the large number of employees who are immigrants. The scope of the operation, however, was unexpected.\par \par \uc2 "I was amazed," she said, adding that she was grateful it didn't involve her teammates. "I just thought, 'It wasn't us -- this time.' "\par \par \uc2 LG Energy also said it was halting most business trips to the U.S. and directing employees on assignment in the U.S. to return home immediately or remain in their accommodations. Meanwhile, Hyundai has urged caution to employees traveling to the U.S., an industry official said.\par \par \uc2 U.S. authorities have said those arrested had illegally crossed the border, entered through a visa-waiver program that prohibited them from working or had overstayed their visas. The initial targets were four Hispanic workers, according to the search warrant, which was unsealed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The raid caught Seoul and Hyundai by surprise and has rattled South Korea, a close Washington ally and top trading partner. Seoul wasn't given prior notice of the raid by U.S. officials, a South Korean official said.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean nationals were largely given visas suitable for training purposes, such as the B-1 visa, and many there were working as instructors, a South Korean government official said.\par \par \uc2 South Korean Foreign Minister Cho Hyun will travel to Washington on Monday, seeking to facilitate the release of the detained workers and hash out administrative procedures ahead of their return home. Cho also plans to ask for Trump administration cooperation to prevent similar incidents in the future. That includes discussions around potential changes to the visa system for Korean workers sent to the U.S. to assist with American investment projects.\par \par \uc2 South Korea previously had vocalized frustration over the lack of visas reserved for the country that would make it easier to send its skilled workers to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Hyundai has bet big on expanding its U.S. footprint. The site where the Thursday raid occurred represents the state's largest-ever manufacturing investment, Georgia officials say.\par \par \uc2 Once the Georgia complex is complete, Hyundai says it will employ 8,500 workers and have the capacity to make a half million vehicles a year. In late March, the company officially opened the plant with a ceremony featuring Republican Gov. Brian Kemp, who called the project a "once-in-a-generation opportunity, made possible by our commitment to working with job creators to build fruitful partnerships for decades."\par \par \uc2 The heap of praise was followed by a string of months where Hyundai, which also owns the \uc2 Genesis\uc2  luxury brand and has a major stake in rival \uc2 Kia\uc2 , largely refrained from imposing price increases on its models and chose to absorb Trump's auto tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the company reported record U.S. monthly sales for August, and an executive said it plans to keep growing, no matter what "comes out of left field," be it a pandemic, a microchip shortage or new, costly tariffs.\par \par \uc2 "Really the best way to beat the tariffs is to continue to invest in the United States," said Randy Parker, CEO of \uc2 Hyundai Motor America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250908el980001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
