\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Illegal School Strikes in Washington\b0\par\par\uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Class was supposed to start more than a week ago at Evergreen Public Schools in Vancouver, Wash., one of the state's biggest K-12 districts, with about 22,000 students. Instead some 1,400 bus drivers, custodians, aides and other staff went on strike. It isn't lawful in Washington, but this chaos is what happens if government unions don't have to pay a price for walking off the job.\par \par \uc2 The district's support workers, some of them part time, are represented by an affiliate of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 . It's demanding up to 15% raises over three years for some staff, according to Oregon Public Broadcasting. The district offered 9% to 12.5% over three years, but the union voted Thursday to reject the proposal. Meanwhile, the district says it faces a $26 million budget hole.\par \par \uc2 Rather than strike, the union members could have kept working for a year under the last contract's terms, according to the district. Washington law doesn't protect strikes by public employees, and court rulings have said they're illegal. "No such right existed at common law, and none has been granted by statute," said a 2006 state Attorney General opinion. Alas, the statute books "do not impose penalties on public employees for engaging in a strike."\par \par \uc2 As a result, unions do it regardless of the legality. "Washington has seen 78 school districts strike since 1976," the Columbian newspaper said in 2018. "Injunctions were filed in 25 of those," but in 12 of them "teachers defied the injunctions." Unions calculate that getting a hefty contract might outweigh any penalty imposed by a judge. After a 2018 strike, Evergreen teachers nabbed a salary increase of 11.5%.\par \par \uc2 The state Legislature could change the calculus by adding penalties for public union strikes, but Washington politics is union friendly. This spring lawmakers passed a bill, effective in January, to let striking workers collect unemployment benefits, though the money must be paid back if a court deems the walkout illegal.\par \par \uc2 The Evergreen school board was scheduled Friday to weigh a resolution authorizing legal action against the union "to avoid irreparable injury to the educational process." Meantime, up the highway in La Center, Wash., schools have been closed since Wednesday thanks to a teachers strike. Support staff at Vancouver Public Schools could vote Sept. 11 to authorize a strike if they don't get a contract deal. The Mead School District near Spokane barely averted a teacher strike this week.\par \par \uc2 When this un-public-spirited behavior is indulged, the losers are the parents, students, and teachers who want to get the school year going.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250906el960001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
