\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Revisions Show Job Losses in June\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan \par \uc2 286 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy in June shed jobs for the first month since December 2020, according to revised payroll figures from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Overall revisions to nonfarm payrolls were smaller than in the previous jobs report, when the \uc2 BLS\uc2  slashed its estimate of total employment for May and June by 258,000 and prompted President Trump to fire the agency's commissioner.\par \par \uc2 Still, they came in negative. The \uc2 BLS\uc2  said Friday the economy added 21,000 fewer jobs in June and July than it had previously estimated.\par \par \uc2 That left the third and final estimate for total job creation in June at minus 13,000.\par \par \uc2 Between January and June, the \uc2 BLS\uc2 's final estimates of nonfarm payrolls have deviated from its initial estimates by an average of 81,000 per month. In 2023 and 2024, the \uc2 BLS\uc2 's initial payroll estimates were off by an average of 51,000 and 48,000, respectively.\par \par \uc2 The revisions remain small relative to the total number of nonfarm jobs in the U.S. -- 159.5 million as of August. But they can be significant relative to the month-on-month change in payrolls, which is what markets tend to focus on. That is especially the case now, given that job creation appears to be slowing.\par \par \uc2 Revisions happen because many of the businesses and government agencies that the \uc2 BLS\uc2  surveys each month don't report data in time for that month's jobs report. Tweaks by \uc2 BLS\uc2  statisticians to seasonal adjustments can also be a factor.\par \par \uc2 Among major industries, June employment figures were revised down for professional and business services, leisure and hospitality, and construction, among others.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich487\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7305
