\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Electric-Vehicle Sales Drive Auto Market --- The momentum isn't expected to last, however, as tax credits are expiring\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Otts \par \uc2 598 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Electric vehicles are propelling auto sales in the U.S. -- but not for long.\par \par \uc2 New-vehicle sales rose 3.7% in August from a year earlier, helped by consumers' rush to lock in expiring EV tax credits and steady prices in the face of tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , Hyundai and \uc2 Kia\uc2  this week reported double-digit increases from a year earlier, while \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  reported gains across all powertrains and body styles. Overall sales industry-wide jumped 3.7% in the month from a year earlier to a seasonally adjusted annual selling rate for August of 16.4 million vehicles, according to Motor Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Electric vehicles and plug-in hybrids were bright spots for most vehicle makers in August as consumers raced to take advantage of $7,500 federal tax credits that expire Sept. 30. Electric models comprised more than 11% of the U.S. new-vehicle market for the month compared with the 8% share they usually have, said Tyson Jominy, vice president of data and analytics at \uc2 J.D. Power\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the surge in EV sales for the month is expected to peter out after the tax credit expires. Several automakers are already taking steps to get ahead of that decline, laying off workers and cutting production of electric models.\par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  is cutting a shift for six months starting in December at a plant in Tennessee that assembles electric Cadillac SUVs, and is planning to start production of its new Chevrolet Bolt EV in Kansas City this year with only one shift instead of two, a person familiar with the plan said.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 General Motors\uc2  is making strategic production adjustments in alignment with expected slower EV industry growth and customer demand," a company spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  is planning to slow production at a Tennessee plant and temporarily furlough 160 workers. VW made the decision to align production volumes with market demand, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Electric-vehicle maker \uc2 Rivian\uc2  on Thursday laid off over 200 hundred employees as the company is trying to reduce costs ahead of the launch of a more affordable sport-utility vehicle next year.\par \par \uc2 The auto industry overall has had a year of turmoil with tariffs shaving $12 billion and counting from their bottom lines and Trump's regulatory shifts deflating the outlook for electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Companies have been sacrificing profits to maintain their respective shares of the market, while hoping Trump reaches deals to alleviate the levies. They also are looking at savings from Trump's evisceration of fuel-economy regulations.\par \par \uc2 Randy Parker, CEO of Hyundai Motor North America, said that while demand for EVs is expected to drop in the fourth quarter, the company remains optimistic about the technology. "I do believe that once the market settles, the EV market will continue to grow," he said.\par \par \uc2 Hyundai brand EV sales rose 72% in August to 10,165, led by the Ioniq 5. While \uc2 Honda\uc2 's overall U.S. sales slid 5% in the month, the brand's electric Prologue SUV soared nearly 80% to its best month, the automaker said.\par \par \uc2 GM said it sold a record 21,000 EVs in August across its Chevrolet, Cadillac and GMC brands, up from the previous record of 19,000 in July. GM is estimated to have sold 6% more vehicles overall, with EVs accounting for less than 9% of the total, according to Motor Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Ford's electric Mustang posted a monthly sales record, helping its overall EV sales to rise 19%.\par \