\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: EV Maker \uc2 Rivian\uc2  Cuts Workers as It Preps Cheaper SUV\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ryan Felton \par \uc2 424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rivian\uc2  is laying off workers as the company is trying to reduce costs ahead of the launch of a more affordable sport-utility vehicle next year.\par \par \uc2 The cuts represent less than 1.5% of \uc2 Rivian\uc2 's workforce, a spokesperson said. The company had about 15,000 employees at the end of last year.\par \par \uc2 "We have made some recent changes to the commercial team as part of an ongoing effort to improve operational efficiency for R2," a \uc2 Rivian\uc2  spokesperson said. The commercial team handles sales and servicing operations for the company.\par \par \uc2 Affected employees are eligible for rehire and encouraged to apply for other open positions, the spokesperson added. The workforce reductions took effect on Thursday for U.S. and Canadian workers.\par \par \uc2 U.S. carmakers have been bracing for the end of a $7,500 federal tax credit for EVs that expires at the end of the month, with many production slowdowns already being planned. At the same time, automotive-policy changes have disrupted the balance sheets of EV-only car companies such as \uc2 Rivian\uc2  by derailing sales of compliance credits to competitors needing to meet fuel economy and emission standards.\par \par \uc2 Rivian\uc2 , which went public in 2021, has been under pressure to lower costs while it prepares the launch of its next model, the R2 SUV, which is expected to have a starting price around $45,000.\par \par \uc2 The company has said it has enough money to launch the R2, despite incurring a loss of $1.1 billion in the second quarter. \uc2 Rivian\uc2  expects higher demand for R2 than for its current R1T pickup and R1S SUV, which start at $70,990 and $76,900, respectively.\par \par \uc2 The automaker has been hit by regulatory changes under the Trump administration, including the elimination of fines for violations of the Corporate Average Fuel Economy rule. In a recent court filing, the company said the changes could result in an estimated $100 million in revenue being held up.\par \par \uc2 Automakers have been preparing to reduce production on EVs after the tax credit winds down Sept. 30. \uc2 General Motors\uc2  recently said it laid off about 360 employees at a plant in Detroit for at least a month to reduce production of the GMC Hummer EV and battery-powered Cadillac Escalade IQ.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  said this week it would slow production of the electric ID.4 SUV at its plant in Tennessee and temporarily furlough 160 workers. VW made the decision to align production volumes with market demand, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250905el950000s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
