\par\par \b \uc2 Personal-Injury Lawyers Add Data-Breach Suits to Docket --- Cyberattack surge has some litigators vying for victims seeking big payouts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Angus Loten \par \uc2 887 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A growing number of personal-injury lawyers are adding data-breach lawsuits to caseloads, alongside traffic accidents, medical malpractice and dog bites.\par \par \uc2 The upswing is being fueled by a surge in cyberattacks, as hackers become more sophisticated at breaking into business systems with an ever-increasing cache of customer data.\par \par \uc2 Driven by the rise of AI-powered phishing attacks, U.S. companies reported over 1,700 data breaches in the first half of 2025, compared with a full-year total of 3,155 breaches reported in 2024, according to the Identity Theft Resource Center.\par \par \uc2 This year, companies have issued more than 165 million individual victim notifications, the nonprofit research firm said.\par \par \uc2 The deluge has personal-injury law firms chasing plaintiffs within hours of a company's data-breach notification, taking to \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , TikTok and other social-media platforms, lawyers and cybersecurity experts said.\par \par \uc2 To get ahead of the pack, many new data-breach lawyers are equipped with online cyberattack alerts, customizable ad templates and ready-made legal paperwork, they said.\par \par \uc2 "Lawyers are an opportunistic bunch," \uc2 Thomas Loeser\uc2 , a partner at Cotchett, Pitre & McCarthy, said about the rush to file lawsuits after companies issue a data-breach notification. "We have seen a tremendous increase in firms that take on data-breach cases," he said, citing firms that file six to seven cases a week.\par \par \uc2 Last year, U.S. lawyers filed 1,488 class-action lawsuits related to data breaches, up from 1,320 in 2023 and just 604 in 2022, according to law firm Duane Morris.\par \par \uc2 Car accidents and medical malpractice remain the two biggest moneymakers for the more than 50,000 personal injury law firms across the U.S., according to research firm IBISWorld.\par \par \uc2 But data breaches are one of the fastest-growing areas of class action litigation, said James Turgal, vice president of global cyber advisory, risk and board relations at \uc2 Optiv\uc2 , a cyber advisory firm.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers are tallying big wins.\par \par \uc2 AT&T\uc2  in June reached a $177 million preliminary settlement with customers caught up in a pair of data breaches disclosed last year. The telecom giant faced a flood of personal and class-action suits after notifying customers about the attacks, including a breach that enabled hackers to download call and text-message information from nearly all of its 90 million wireless subscribers. The court's final approval is expected in December.\par \par \uc2 Other recent class-action data-breach lawsuits include \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2 , which in June settled for $45 million, and \uc2 Neiman Marcus\uc2 , which in May settled for $3.5 million.\par \par \uc2 Early settlements can help companies avoid costly, drawn-out legal battles that can leave lasting reputational damage.\par \par \uc2 But they can also drain cyber insurance funds meant to shore up a company's data safeguards, said Megan Silverman, vice president of cyber solutions at \uc2 Integreon\uc2 , a firm that provides legal, business and creative services to global companies.\par \par \uc2 "Instead of investing those dollars in stronger data protections, much of the money is now flowing to plaintiffs' attorneys and their clients," Silverman said.\par \par \uc2 Companies operating with "truly egregious practices" -- like not having multifactor authentication or relying on shared standardized passwords -- are fair game for plaintiffs, Silverman said. But companies that follow accepted cybersecurity practices and still get breached shouldn't be facing lawsuits, she said.\par \par \uc2 "Anyone can be breached if the threat actors try hard and long enough," she said.\par \par \uc2 Craig Hoffman, partner and co-leader of \uc2 Baker & Hostetler\uc2 's Digital Risk Advisory and Cybersecurity team, said over the past few years the likelihood of a company getting hit with at least one class-action lawsuit after disclosing a security incident has jumped.\par \par \uc2 In the past five years, he said, \uc2 Baker & Hostetler\uc2  has led clients through roughly 1,250 data breaches every year, resulting in about 500 situations that require notification per local, federal or industry regulations.\par \par \uc2 Last year, more than 50 notices sparked lawsuits, compared with just 20 in 2020, Hoffman said.\par \par \uc2 "If you go back five to 10 years, you would normally only expect a lawsuit if you were notifying hundreds of thousands or millions of people," Hoffman said.\par \par \uc2 Today, data-breach lawsuits can involve 1,000 or fewer individuals, he said.\par \par \uc2 The sheer number of law firms vying for a piece of a data-breach case can bog down the legal process.\par \par \uc2 "It's such a crowded space now," said Amina Thomas, partner on the class-action team at CohenMalad, an Indiana-based law firm that has handled data-breach suits for nearly a decade.\par \par \uc2 More than a half-dozen attorneys with their own plaintiffs can battle to lead the same case, adding weeks and even months to proceedings.\par \par \uc2 In cases involving large companies or widespread leaks, multiple law firms often file their own unique lawsuits and compete with each other to lead a case, Cotchett, Pitre & McCarthy's Loeser said.\par \par \uc2 "It does make it harder," he said.\par \par \uc2 Loeser, a former federal cyber prosecutor, is one of 10 lawyers appointed to the leadership of the \uc2 AT&T\uc2  lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 It used to be that taking on a data-breach case was risky and expensive, he said: "There are now firms that have a volume practice."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Angus Loten writes about cybersecurity for WSJ Pro.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250904el9400017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
