\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 California's Ride-Share Stick-Up\b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 California Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  on Friday announced a "deal" with ride-share companies \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  that they couldn't refuse. Democrats in Sacramento will reduce auto insurance coverage mandates that are driving runaway litigation in return for the companies letting drivers collectively bargain.\par \par \uc2 Unions have long sought to unionize California's some 800,000 ride-share drivers and now may finally succeed thanks to Democrats. "This is a historic agreement between workers and business that only California could deliver," Mr. Newsom said. True. Only California Democrats would extort ride-share drivers and riders to reward their union friends.\par \par \uc2 Voters in 2020 enacted Prop. 22, which defines ride-share drivers as independent contractors and provides them with various benefits. Independent contractors aren't allowed to unionize under federal antitrust and labor law. But Democrats devised a crafty end-run that invokes the "state action antitrust exemption" to grease the wheels for unionization.\par \par \uc2 The legislative deal would allow unions to petition the state's Public Employment Relations Board for certification. Voila, unions can organize drivers free from antitrust liability because of state oversight. The precise language of the legislation was still being drafted as we wrote this, but we're told it won't diminish benefits drivers receive under Prop. 22.\par \par \uc2 Democrats' move to let drivers unionize might have drawn a legal challenge from ride-share companies. But Democrats agreed in return to enact modest reforms to the state's driver insurance coverage mandates, which invite lawsuits and increase fares. In most of the country, mandatory insurance costs account for 10% or less of the average \uc2 Uber\uc2  fare. They make up 32% in California and 45% in Los Angeles County.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Newsom said the deal will make "rideshare more affordable for millions of Californians." Maybe if collective-bargaining agreements don't drive up driver costs. But Republicans in states like Florida, Georgia and Louisiana have enacted legal and insurance reforms without requiring anything in return from business. Democrats in Sacramento are once again taking politically captive businesses for a ride.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250903el9300018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
