\par\par \b \uc2 How Tariffs Hurt My American Factory\b0\par\par\uc2 By George Matouk Jr. \par \uc2 566 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As 50% tariffs take effect Wednesday on products and manufacturing materials imported from India, Americans should know what these levies are doing to U.S. companies like my family's.\par \par \uc2 Matouk is an American maker of luxury bed and bath linens, founded by my grandfather in 1929. While much of our industry has abandoned domestic production, we have built a profitable production operation in Fall River, Mass., where we have nearly 200 manufacturing employees.\par \par \uc2 Ironically, these new, steep tariffs will damage our domestic manufacturing operation and make us less competitive than some rival companies that rely only on foreign production. Over the past 20 years, we have invested consistently in our American operation, which depends on a global supply chain that delivers material and equipment for production to Fall River. These fabrics and machines aren't available in the U.S. and, in my opinion, never will be. The majority of our competitors produce their goods entirely abroad and often in countries that are now more tariff-favored than India, where we buy many of our inputs. This means our American-produced goods are at a disadvantage, due to tariffs.\par \par \uc2 To supplement our domestically made product line, we also import products that are impossible to manufacture economically in America (by us or anyone else) because the skills and materials don't exist here or because the costs are too high. We produce these items with world class companies in countries that now have burdensome additional tariffs. These include the Philippines (additional 19%), India (additional 50%), Turkey (additional 15%) and Liechtenstein (additional 15%). The products we buy in these countries make Matouk a stronger company, provide enhanced choices to American consumers, and help our U.S. hotel and retail partners and all the people they employ.\par \par \uc2 There's no way our overseas manufacturers can absorb these high taxes -- which is what tariffs are. The textile industry is one of the world's most competitive: Textiles are produced in nearly every country, and all compete to sell in the U.S. Producers operate with consistently low profit margins, which leave them with little room to share tariffs' costs. American manufacturers and importers must shoulder almost the entire burden. While we have borne these costs since Liberation Day in hope that the administration would choose a more reasonable policy by the Aug. 1 implementation deadline, we now reluctantly have to raise our prices to protect our business.\par \par \uc2 Matouk is an innovative company that has existed for nearly 100 years. We will find a way to overcome what feels like an attack by our own government. We aren't looking for a trophy for our commitment to American production. But we would ask for a sensible tariff policy that would benefit a company that has invested in domestic manufacturing, against the tide of our industry. Instead, we're being punished for sticking with U.S. manufacturing -- as are our employees, the businesses we sell to, and ultimately American consumers, who share in the cost of these higher taxes.\par \par \uc2 On behalf of Matouk and many other innovative U.S. companies punished by these tariffs, I ask the Trump administration to do better -- and fast. Time is running out before the White House inflicts lasting damage to our industrial base and its best companies.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Matouk is CEO of John Matouk & Co.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250903el930000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
