\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Slowing Job Market Hits Black Americans First\b0\par\par\uc2 By Konrad Putzier and Rachel Wolfe \par \uc2 771 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unemployment in the U.S. remains historically low at 4.2%. Yet Black workers are living in a different reality: Unemployment for Black Americans has surged to 7.2%, the highest level since October 2021, when the job market was still recovering from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The drop in employment is a warning sign for the labor market and threatens to unravel employment gains made by Black workers during and after the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Seasonally adjusted unemployment is up significantly for college-educated and non-college-educated Black workers. This coincides with a general slowdown in the labor market that has locked many workers out of the job market for months. Black workers have borne the brunt of that downturn, according to economists, in a reflection of entrenched racial disparities.\par \par \uc2 Black workers are more likely to hold low-skilled and junior-level jobs than their white counterparts, making them more vulnerable to layoffs. They have long faced discrimination in the labor market that can become more pronounced when overall hiring slows, as it has in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Also, a recent increase in unemployment among Black college graduates points to the possible impact of federal job cuts. The federal workforce has a disproportionate share of Black workers.\par \par \uc2 "I am in the fight of my life now," said Kenya Jenkins, 52 years old, who has been actively looking for work since being laid off from her job as a contractor for the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  in December.\par \par \uc2 Jenkins, who has a master's degree in human services and public health, had to leave her apartment in Maryland as a result and move in with relatives in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Overall unemployment remains low, but with hiring slowed to a trickle, it is a labor market with little margin for error. Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  recently indicated that the job market has cooled enough to potentially justify interest-rate cuts in September.\par \par \uc2 Historically, Black workers often lose jobs at the front of a broader labor-market slowdown. "It's a canary in the coal mine for what may be coming," said Kenneth Couch, an economist at the \uc2 University of Connecticut\uc2 . The Labor Department is set to release August's unemployment rate on Friday. The previous report showed employers added relatively few jobs in May, June and July.\par \par \uc2 Kirsten Bradford, 29, has struggled to replace the full-time communications job at a Dallas nonprofit that she lost in January. She said hundreds of applications for an array of jobs have yielded only scattered interviews and no hits.\par \par \uc2 She has been driving around Dallas, stopping at dozens of bank branches to shake hands and hand out her resume, hoping to break into a banking career. The only job she has been able to land is helping customers pick out body sprays and bath bombs at a local \uc2 Bath & Body Works\uc2 , where she earns $14 an hour. She has also continued working for the nonprofit for 20 hours a month.\par \par \uc2 Bradford and her son, 8, recently moved back in with her parents, and she has been fielding calls from collectors on her roughly $100,000 in student debt.\par \par \uc2 "Thinking I did everything right, thinking that every movement I made was for the future just kind of makes me feel so angry," said Bradford, who has a master's degree in management from \uc2 Southern Methodist University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In 2023, the share of Black workers who were unemployed fell to 4.8%, the lowest level since the Labor Department began tracking such data in 1972. The gap between Black and white unemployment shrank to the narrowest on record that year, and Black workers for years saw higher wage gains.\par \par \uc2 Labor shortages meant people could more easily find work, while some companies tried harder to diversify their workforce in the wake of demonstrations following the murder of George Floyd by police in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration recently said that it expects the federal workforce to shrink by 300,000 by December, compared with January of this year.\par \par \uc2 Yvonne Robertson said she was one of around two dozen people of color in a \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  invoice-auditing department who lost their jobs in March. She said she is still being paid while lawsuits play out in court, but is also hunting for a replacement job she expects she will need.\par \par \uc2 It has been challenging, the 56-year-old said. "I'm looking at the competition," said Robertson. "I have two years of college. There are a lot of people who have master's degrees."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich309\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4635
