\par\par \b \uc2 ESPN Employees' Favorite Sport: Lamenting Their Home Base --- To work at the storied sports-media company, it's often Bristol, Conn., or bust\b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabella Simonetti \par \uc2 930 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When \uc2 ESPN\uc2  told employees in March it would be closing its Los Angeles "SportsCenter" operation, it gave about 35 staffers a choice: Move to the network's headquarters in Bristol, Conn., or lose your job. Fewer than half accepted.\par \par \uc2 That was fine with \uc2 ESPN\uc2 . To work at the world's most storied sports-media company, it's often Bristol or bust. And \uc2 ESPN\uc2  isn't going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 "Never," said \uc2 Jimmy Pitaro\uc2 , \uc2 ESPN\uc2 's chairman. "We've never contemplated relocating and I don't have any plans to relocate. Again, we love it here."\par \par \uc2 Employees have lamented living in the central Connecticut city practically since \uc2 ESPN\uc2  was founded in 1979. Decades later, Bristol is often still a punch line. Keith Olbermann, a former anchor, jokingly named it the most "God-forsaken place" on the East Coast during an appearance with former \uc2 ESPN\uc2  colleague Craig Kilborn, the first host of the "The Daily Show." Even Bill Rasmussen, \uc2 ESPN\uc2 's founder, described its roots sardonically: "They took a dump, put fresh grass over it, and called it prime real estate."\par \par \uc2 It was Rasmussen, a Connecticut resident already, who chose Bristol as the network's homebase. It offered the satellite capabilities and land \uc2 ESPN\uc2  needed.\par \par \uc2 Bristol (population 61,129) also boasts carousel and clock museums and is home to a 383-foot elevator test tower. A description on the jobs site for \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 ESPN\uc2 's parent company, highlights the city's "picturesque hills and fall foliage," saying it offers "wonderful memories no matter your pace."\par \par \uc2 "It was a place you went to become a star," said Scott Van Pelt, a veteran anchor who worked in Bristol for about 20 years before relocating to Bethesda, Md. "For a lot of folks, it was the cost of doing business."\par \par \uc2 That was a different era, said Van Pelt, when social media couldn't launch careers and \uc2 ESPN\uc2  was one of the only major outlets for broadcast sports journalists.\par \par \uc2 Still, for those looking to climb the sports-media ladder today, Bristol remains part of the bargain. Employees often work long, odd hours, and living there can create a unique bond.\par \par \uc2 Avoiding Bristol is a privilege mostly reserved for \uc2 ESPN\uc2 's biggest stars. Stephen A. Smith, the more than $100-million face of the network, is based mainly in New York. Pat McAfee, whose \uc2 YouTube\uc2  show \uc2 ESPN\uc2  is licensing for more than $85 million over five years, is based in Indianapolis. Shams Charania, \uc2 ESPN\uc2 's \uc2 NBA\uc2  scoop monster, lives in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Employees getting their start aren't often granted those kinds of privileges. To have fun, they need to get creative.\par \par \uc2 Summer interns live in Homewood Suites hotel rooms in Southington, southeast of Bristol, near a LongHorn Steakhouse and a string of gas stations. Nearby, there's Lake Compounce, which describes itself as "America's first amusement park."\par \par \uc2 ESPN\uc2  visitors often stay in the nearby DoubleTree by Hilton, where they have extra-large beds intended for former athletes. Pitaro said he grew fond of the chain, including its complimentary chocolate chip cookies.\par \par \uc2 "It's definitely not the sexiest place in Connecticut," said Sierra Galanza, 28, who helps run a career-development program at \uc2 ESPN\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Galanza got a mini Australian Shepherd -- named Boomer after the anchor Chris Berman -- early into her time in Bristol. She has built a social life around going to the farmers' market, playing club volleyball and visiting parks.\par \par \uc2 "I think I'll live here throughout my thirties," she said. "I can see myself raising a family here."\par \par \uc2 Another upside: Rent on her two-bedroom is less than $1,100 a month.\par \par \uc2 A downside: wildlife. \uc2 ESPN\uc2 's campus, which sits on more than 100 acres, occasionally gets visits from bears and bobcats. Johanna Goldblatt, a programming and acquisitions manager, sometimes dodges angry Canada geese on campus.\par \par \uc2 "They'll hiss at you," said Goldblatt, 28. \uc2 ESPN\uc2  has contracted a company that uses Border Collies to shoo them away. Goldblatt, who has worked in Bristol for six years, lives nearby with her boyfriend, whom she met at \uc2 ESPN\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Pitaro moved to Connecticut from L.A. in 2018, when he was offered the top job. "It was clear to me that I needed to be here," he said.\par \par \uc2 While Pitaro, who lives an hour south of Bristol, said he and his family love their lives in Connecticut, he conceded it was challenging at first.\par \par \uc2 "I was leading the place, and I felt lonely," said Pitaro.\par \par \uc2 That prompted a conversation with human resources and employee relations about supporting people at work and in Bristol. The company rents out Lake Compounce for an annual picnic and hosts regular town halls, outdoor movie nights and a speaker series featuring celebrities and league commissioners.\par \par \uc2 "There are times when I felt bad for some of the younger women who were like, 'I can't meet anybody here,'" said Laura Rutledge, a football reporter and host. Rutledge, who lived in Bristol from 2022 to 2024 with her husband, said she encourages them to visit New York and Boston.\par \par \uc2 Love in Bristol is possible. A "monstrous" number of people meet their significant others at \uc2 ESPN\uc2 , said Van Pelt, whose wife also worked there.\par \par \uc2 Michael Heimbach, who helps run security for \uc2 ESPN\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2 , said he met his now-wife on his second day, when she was an executive at \uc2 Herman Miller\uc2 , getting a tour of a potential new project on \uc2 ESPN\uc2 's campus.\par \par \uc2 "I said, 'I love your furniture,'" Heimbach said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250902el920001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
