\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 The Wages of Teen Unemployment\b0\par\par\uc2 541 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Artificial intelligence is reducing jobs for young people, or so we keep hearing. But what about the increasing number of teenagers who are priced out of jobs by government? This is something you won't hear politicians talk about this Labor Day, but we thought readers might like to know.\par \par \uc2 Overall U.S. unemployment has remained low over the last year. But drill down into the Labor Department jobs data, and you see that unemployment among 16 to 19 years old has shot up to 15.2% in July from 11.8% in January. The share of teens who are working is the lowest since August 2020, when most states were still locked down amid the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 A softening economy no doubt explains some of the increase in teen joblessness. But another culprit is rising minimum wages in many states. Take California, which has the nation's highest teen unemployment rate, and also one of the highest minimum wages at $16.50 an hour. Democrats enacted a $20 an hour minimum in 2023 for fast-food restaurant chains.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment among teens in California averaged 21.2% last year, about twice the national average. The Labor Department doesn't publish state teen unemployment rates on a monthly basis, but Census Bureau non-seasonally adjusted data showed California's teen jobless rate was a whopping 31% in July, double what it was two years ago before the fast-food minimum took effect.\par \par \uc2 What do you know? When government increases the price of labor, employers use less of it. Teen unemployment rates in Colorado (16.4%), Washington state (14.8%) and Illinois (14.2%) were also among the highest in the country. So are their minimum wages: Washington ($16.66 an hour), Illinois ($15), and Colorado ($14.81).\par \par \uc2 Florida has been adding jobs faster than most states, but its teen unemployment rate (12.1%) last year was roughly the national average. Voters in Florida enacted a referendum in 2020 that raised the state's minimum to $13 an hour this year from $8.56. It is set to increase to $15 in 2026.\par \par \uc2 One outlier is Kentucky, which had the nation's second highest unemployment rate (17.4%) last year despite following the federal $7.25 an hour minimum. But its share of teens who are employed (38%) is higher than in most states because more participate in the labor force. The opposite is true of New York, where fewer teens are unemployed (10.9%) because few are looking for work.\par \par \uc2 Most economic research shows high minimum wages increase teen unemployment, though there are no doubt other contributors. In a tight labor market, wage mandates may have less of an effect on hiring. But when the economy cools and competition for labor weakens, higher minimums make it more costly to hire teens.\par \par \uc2 The upshot is that teens lose chances to learn critical life skills, such as showing up on time, following orders and juggling tasks. Politicians love to claim credit for raising the minimum wage, but they never consider the unintended casualties -- idle young people who will be less equipped for future careers and life.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Progressive Politics Backfire" -- WSJ Sept. 16, 2025)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250830el8u0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
