\par\par \b \uc2 Tech Staff Stage Revolt Over Gaza\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katherine Blunt and Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 991 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Police arrested three former \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  staffers and several other people on Tuesday for protesting at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's headquarters. A Page One article on Thursday about protests at tech companies incorrectly said four former \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  staffers were among those arrested.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ Aug. 30, 2025)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 When a group of employees raised a Palestinian flag outside \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's headquarters and occupied a top executive's office, it marked an escalation of unrest brewing inside Silicon Valley's biggest companies over their work with the Israeli military.\par \par \uc2 While tech giants have long given employees latitude to engage in political speech at work, the latest wave of activism over the war in Gaza has put some workers at odds with their employers. On Wednesday, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  said it had fired two employees who were part of the group that stormed its offices this week. Last year, \uc2 Google\uc2  fired dozens of workers after they staged similar sit-ins.\par \par \uc2 For months, employee discourse about Gaza has tested workplace policies at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2 . Content moderators of internal message boards have repeatedly deleted comments about the conflict, according to current and former employees and internal correspondence reviewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's moderators have deleted questions and conversation threads related to the war in Gaza and the company's work with Israel, screenshots reviewed by the Journal show. On a \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 -wide message board, one employee wrote about the impact of food shortages and attacks on people in Gaza. "How much more must we endure before we say 'enough?'" the person wrote.\par \par \uc2 A content moderator replied with a reminder to follow community guidelines outlining the company's rules for respectful conversation. "We are now closing this thread for comments," the moderator wrote.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, after his office was breached, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  President \uc2 Brad Smith\uc2  said the company is reviewing claims that its technologies had been used to target people in Gaza. The company earlier looked into claims its software was being used to harm people but said it found no evidence.\par \par \uc2 Smith also called the actions of protesters "not standard employee conduct" and said that the company is examining the actions of employee organizers, as well as reviewing its building security protocols. The two now-fired software engineers were arrested by police, along with a former \uc2 Google\uc2  employee and four former \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  staffers.\par \par \uc2 Abdo Mohamed, a former \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  employee who has helped to organize the protests, said he observed numerous instances last year of content moderators intervening when employees asked questions of executives about the company's work in Israel. "\uc2 Microsoft\uc2  really did its best to use the tools at its hands to dismiss any concerns that were brought up," he said.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  said it takes action when content violates company policies and guidelines, and that it creates designated forums for potentially controversial conversations. "We have a responsibility and commitment to creating an inclusive and safe digital workplace," it said.\par \par \uc2 Internal political debate is a complicated matter for companies, especially in industries like tech where employees have a history of activism and engagement. But some companies have gained more leverage over employee exchanges in a shifting labor market, particularly those related to controversial matters.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Google\uc2 , employees have protested military-related contracts and in 2018 convinced the company to quit partnering with the U.S. Defense Department to develop artificial intelligence to identify potential drone targets. Since then, the company has taken steps to curb debates about politics and other controversial matters, in part by appointing employees to moderate conversations.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  last April fired 28 employees who protested the company's $1.2 billion contract with the Israeli government, and then fired another 20 people a few days later. \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  together signed the contract in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Police in New York and California had arrested some of the \uc2 Google\uc2  employees following sit-in protests at its offices. \uc2 Google\uc2  said the employees who were fired disrupted workspaces and made other employees feel unsafe.\par \par \uc2 While discussing the war in Gaza and \uc2 Google\uc2 's cloud-computing contract with the Israeli government, \uc2 Google\uc2  employees who refer to genocide in emails and chat boards have had their conversation threads locked or deleted, according to screenshots reviewed by the Journal. Some received messages from content moderators explaining why.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  said it is routine practice for its content moderation team to remove posts that violate company policies on disruptive content, which includes a range of topics in addition to genocide.\par \par \uc2 "We've been clear that our workplace isn't somewhere to debate politics or have disruptive discussions -- on any divisive topic," said spokeswoman Courtenay Mencini. "We've consistently reminded our employees of this and regularly take steps to enforce the rare instances where employees violate our policies."\par \par \uc2 Many of the moderation instances center on use of the word "genocide." How and when to use the term has remained a subject of fierce debate, and its application to conflicts in Armenia, Rwanda and elsewhere have been controversial.\par \par \uc2 The current war in Gaza was triggered by the October 2023 attack on Israel by Palestinian Islamist militant group \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , which killed about 1,200 people, mostly Israeli citizens. More than 60,000 Palestinians have been killed in Gaza by Israel since the onset of the war, Gaza's Health Ministry has said.\par \par \uc2 When a \uc2 Google\uc2  employee wrote "genocide" in a chat last month, co-founder \uc2 Sergey Brin\uc2  intervened with a sharp warning, according to screenshots reviewed by the Journal. The interaction was earlier reported by \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The conversation touched on a recent report from a United Nations Special Rapporteur that said work by a range of corporations, including \uc2 Google\uc2 , were sustaining Israel's war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , which it called a "genocidal campaign."\par \par \uc2 "The only bright spot here is that we're spending all of our time and money on Gemini and not something more useful for genocide," the employee wrote, referring to \uc2 Google\uc2 's AI model and chatbot.\par \par \uc2 Brin responded: "With all due respect, throwing around the term genocide is deeply offensive to many people who have suffered actual genocides. I would also be careful citing transparently antisemitic organizations like the \uc2 UN\uc2  in relation to these issues."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250828el8s0001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
