\par\par \b \uc2 AI Workers in Their 20s Rake In High Salaries\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katherine Bindley \par \uc2 853 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's a tough time to be a young person looking for a job -- unless you're in artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 The job market for entry-level workers is in a continued slump. The unemployment rate for new college graduates was 4.8% in June, according to the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2 , compared with 4% for all workers.\par \par \uc2 While AI is part of the reason for the doldrums, there is a bright spot when it comes to workers with actual experience in machine learning. They're in their early 20s, they have AI know-how, and a bunch of them are making $1 million a year.\par \par \uc2 Base salaries for nonmanagerial workers in AI with zero to three years experience grew by around 12% from 2024 to 2025, the largest gain of any experience group, according to a new report by the AI staffing firm Burtch Works, which analyzed the compensation of thousands of AI and data-science candidates. The report also found that people with AI experience are being promoted to management roles roughly twice as fast as their counterparts in other technology fields. They're jumping the ladder as a result of their skills and impact instead of their years on the job.\par \par \uc2 "There is a significant salary difference between a machine-learning engineer job and a software-engineer job," said Anil K. Gupta, a professor at the University of Maryland's Robert H. Smith School of Business and co-lead of its AI job tracker.\par \par \uc2 Databricks\uc2 , the data-analytics software company whose value has skyrocketed during the AI boom, plans to triple the number of people it hires right out of school this year, in part because of their familiarity with AI.\par \par \uc2 "They're going to come in, and they're going to be all AI-native," said \uc2 Ali Ghodsi\uc2 , the company's chief executive. "We can't for the life of us get the more senior people to adopt it."\par \par \uc2 A generative-AI research scientist with as little as two years experience can make base salaries between $190,000 and $260,000 at \uc2 Databricks\uc2 , according to the company's job-postings page. Including stock grants, the overall compensation can be much higher.\par \par \uc2 "We definitely have people, quite junior people, that have big impact, and they're getting paid a lot," Ghodsi said. "Under 25, you can be making a million."\par \par \uc2 Even in the marketplace for people with AI experience, there is a bifurcation, said Jure Leskovec, a professor of computer science at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  and co-founder of the startup Kumo.AI. He sees Ph.D. students in their early 20s produce effective research, leave their programs early and get snatched up by companies with large offers before they have any industry experience.\par \par \uc2 "The number of zeros is quite large," he said.\par \par \uc2 Then there are those who are adept at using AI. They learn fast, think fast and are able to leverage the technology to be more effective. Though they don't have the advanced degrees, the gap between them and conventional programmers is widening, he said.\par \par \uc2 "It's almost like a next generation of a software engineer," he said.\par \par \uc2 Many jobs for machine-learning engineers that require zero to a year of experience pay upward of $200,000 a year at companies like \uc2 Roblox\uc2 , according to Levels.fyi. The compensation-data provider has seen 42 user-submitted offers of over $1 million from AI companies. Of those, nine candidates had less than a decade of corporate workplace experience, though some might have had Ph.D.s.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Scale AI\uc2 , which recently underwent a reverse-acquihire deal with \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2 , around 15% of employees are under the age of 25. Right out of school, employees at \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  can expect base salaries of around $200,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 "We're eager to hire AI-native professionals, and many of those candidates are early in their careers," said Ashli Shiftan, \uc2 Scale AI\uc2 's head of people.\par \par \uc2 The company has gone as far as to threaten legal action against firms trying to poach its youngest talent.\par \par \uc2 Lily Ma, after graduating in December with an AI-concentration computer-science major from \uc2 Carnegie Mellon University\uc2 , applied for 30 to 40 jobs. She had interviews with about a dozen. "I did notice that having research experience helps a lot," she said. (She also interned at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 The 22-year-old landed at \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  but turned down some tempting offers, including from a startup that offered a 1% stake in the company.\par \par \uc2 Cyril Gorlla co-founded CTGT, a startup that makes safety and enterprise-risk software designed to fix such problems as AI hallucinations. Its investors include \uc2 Google\uc2 . He said the average age at his company is 21. Some of its employees' equity is now worth about $500,000.\par \par \uc2 Gorlla, who himself is 23, has been receiving inbound requests from younger and younger prospective employees, including a 16-year-old who already had a paper published at an AI conference.\par \par \uc2 "I definitely did not see that a few years ago," he said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7200
