\par\par \b \uc2 Protesters Occupy \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  President's Office\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 234 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  is weighing disciplinary measures for employees who occupied President \uc2 Brad Smith\uc2 's office on Tuesday in protest of the company's relationship with the Israeli government during its war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 The employees, members of a group that calls itself No Azure for Apartheid, broadcast live video of themselves entering Building 34 on \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's Redmond, Wash., campus and hanging banners inside Smith's office.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the group confirmed that several \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  employees participated in the takeover of Smith's office and were arrested.\par \par \uc2 Current and former employees, together with outside protesters, have in recent months engaged in a series of actions that have sought to draw attention to the tech giant's selling of software to the Israeli military, with protesters saying the technology has been used to target civilians in Gaza. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  has said it is investigating the matter.\par \par \uc2 Smith said in a livestreamed press briefing that the company is examining the actions of the employees, which he called "not standard employee conduct." \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  temporarily locked down the building in response to the incident.\par \par \uc2 Smith, who is also \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's vice chair, said the company primarily provides the Israeli military with cybersecurity help.\par \par \uc2 He also said that a company security official in April contacted the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  to ask about "disruptions" that could be planned in the Seattle area.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250827el8r0001z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pi