\par\par \b \uc2 Musk's \uc2 xAI\uc2  Sues \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 OpenAI\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rolfe Winkler \par \uc2 739 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's artificial-intelligence startup \uc2 xAI\uc2  sued \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  on Monday, alleging the companies are illegally thwarting competition for AI companies.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit says the iPhone-maker's partnership with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  makes the startup's ChatGPT the "only generative AI chatbot that benefits from billions of user prompts originating from hundreds of millions of iPhones."\par \par \uc2 That enables \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  to use the prompts and feedback to improve its model, a significant advantage, according to the complaint. The suit also says \uc2 Apple\uc2  is deprioritizing the apps of competing chatbots in its App Store rankings.\par \par \uc2 "In a desperate bid to protect its smartphone monopoly, \uc2 Apple\uc2  has joined forces with the company that most benefits from inhibiting competition and innovation in AI: \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , a monopolist in the market for generative AI chatbots," the lawsuit says.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 Apple\uc2  spokesman didn't respond to a request for comment. \uc2 Apple\uc2  has previously said the App store is designed to be free of bias, and the company has defended the fairness of its partnership with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 . \uc2 Apple\uc2  executives have said they would only partner with what they see as the best products to provide the best user experience.\par \par \uc2 "This latest filing is consistent with Mr. Musk's ongoing pattern of harassment," an \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  spokesman said. Musk has separately sued \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  to try to prevent the startup from converting into a for-profit company, saying it abandoned its mission to give priority to benefits for humanity in developing AI.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit calls attention to \uc2 Apple\uc2 's role as a primary delivery mechanism for AI models as they begin to reshape industries and the global economy. Musk's legal pressure might allow him to elbow his way into a possible distribution agreement akin to \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's and greater promotion for his company in \uc2 Apple\uc2 's App Store by forcing the iPhone-maker to avoid the appearance of unfair treatment.\par \par \uc2 The suit appears to mimic the Justice Department's successful antitrust case against \uc2 Google\uc2 . A federal judge found that \uc2 Google\uc2  illegally monopolized the market for conventional search results, in part by locking up distribution through iPhones. \uc2 xAI\uc2  argues that \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  is doing something similar for AI-driven search.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  pays \uc2 Apple\uc2  more than $20 billion a year to be the default search provider on the Safari browser. The judge is expected to rule in the coming days about potential ways to improve competition and could ban such payments. That case also closely reviewed \uc2 Apple\uc2 's potential AI partnerships.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 xAI\uc2  lawsuit calls \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  "monopolists" and alleges the companies are threatened by \uc2 xAI\uc2  and effectively working together to prevent fair competition. The complaint says that AI poses an "existential threat" to \uc2 Apple\uc2 's business and alleges the company is worried about competition from so-called "super-apps" that provide many kinds of functionality, reducing dependence on smartphones. Musk has sought to make X into such a "super-app" with limited success.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  is protecting its relationship with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  in part by preventing better promotion for \uc2 xAI\uc2 's rival apps, in particular its Grok chatbot and the X social-media app, the suit alleges. The suit claims this helps \uc2 Apple\uc2  by preventing AI companies from achieving greater scale that would make their services more useful and potentially threaten its smartphone business.\par \par \uc2 One hurdle the lawsuit might face is to explain why \uc2 Apple\uc2  would promote \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  -- the market leader for artificial intelligence by users -- if \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  presents a potentially greater threat than Musk's companies to \uc2 Apple\uc2 's business. \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  acquired the company of former \uc2 Apple\uc2  executive Jony Ive hoping he could help develop a next-generation device that weans consumers off smartphones.\par \par \uc2 Super-apps have never caught on in the U.S. the way they have elsewhere. In China, the \uc2 WeChat\uc2  app from Tencent has more than one billion users and provides a number of services -- messaging, social media, food delivery and taxi bookings, for instance -- that U.S. consumers rely on multiple apps to provide.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  has acknowledged the threat AI poses to its smartphone business. Testifying at the \uc2 Google\uc2  antitrust trial in May, \uc2 Apple\uc2  executive Eddy Cue said the continued advance of technology threatens even the most dominant companies. "You may not need an iPhone in 10 years, as crazy as that sounds," he said.\par \par \uc2 News Corp\uc2 , owner of The Wall Street Journal, has a commercial agreement to supply news through \uc2 Apple\uc2  services and a content-licensing partnership with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250826el8q0001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
