\par\par \b \uc2 Travel (A Special Report) --- Vacations for the Nerd in You: Scholarly travel courses offer the intellectually curious the chance to combine tourism and learning. The syllabus isn't exactly beach reading.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brigid Mander \par \uc2 889 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some vacationers pine for naps on a beach, or thrill to ever-expanding hotel amenities. For Don Phillips, excitement about coming travel ramps up when he receives the reading list.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, Phillips has traveled to England's University of Oxford to study Dickens and Celtic literature, and to Athens and Thessaloniki to study classic Greek texts. He is currently diving into the works of James Joyce in preparation for a weeklong Joyce course in Dublin.\par \par \uc2 "It's nerdy, I admit it," says the 63-year-old Phillips, a managing director at investment-research firm \uc2 Morningstar\uc2  in Chicago. "But there's such a unique connection to place when you're traveling in a learning environment."\par \par \uc2 Modern tourism with its crowds and commercialism can be a turnoff for travelers seeking deeper meaning. Enter scholarly travel courses offered by university extension programs and lifelong-learning centers. Most groups are small -- around 20 people or fewer -- and don't require alumni affiliation or coursework. But groups tend to be made up of intellectually curious people who read the suggested materials and engage on topics.\par \par \uc2 Phillips travels with the University of Chicago's Graham School, which offers noncredit liberal-arts classes to adults. "The focus isn't prestige or fancy hotels," he says. "It's about friendship, dialogue and learning."\par \par \uc2 Starr Osborne, a 62-year-old avid traveler from Philadelphia, takes similar trips run by Cornell University's Adult University. Osborne has studied arctic and interior Alaska and plans to join a history course in Chile. "I love a good beach, but travel with professors with years of study in a place or topic brings a really sophisticated layer of interest and understanding," says Osborne.\par \par \uc2 Typical travelers in these groups are motivated to learn, says Steve Thaxton, executive director of the National Resource Center for Osher Lifelong Learning Institutes, based at \uc2 Northwestern University\uc2 . Osher endows and provides resources for lifelong learning at more than 120 institutions. Thaxton says target travel-study participants tend to be people "who'd rather visit unconventional locations with an academic approach. They enjoy experiencing places in a way you can't necessarily do with typical group travel."\par \par \uc2 Here is a sampling of travel for the academically curious:\par \par \uc2 Fortnight in Oxford\par \par \uc2 Immerse yourself in English literature or history on one of the Graham School's two-week visits to the \uc2 University of Oxford\uc2 , one of which focuses on literature, the other on history. Recent trips, which enlist Oxford faculty, have focused on Elizabethan and Stuart England and on Shakespeare's romantic works. Participants stay in Oxford student housing but don't attend regular classes. Extracurriculars have included visits to special collections at Oxford's Bodleian Libraries to see rare manuscripts such as original source books used by Shakespeare from the 1500s and dinner at the Oxford Sports Club. Participants receive a certificate from Oxford's continuing education department. A one-week, or sennight, option is also offered.\par \par \uc2 Cost for two weeks: From $7,000\par \par \uc2 Madagascar: Lemurs, Chameleons & Baobabs\par \par \uc2 Cornell's Adult University offers an intense field trip with two professors from Cornell's Department of Entomology: Linda Rayor, a behavioral ecologist specializing in arachnids and primates; and Cole Gilbert, a biologist specializing in the neurobiological basis of insect behavior. The professors are also well-versed in larger fauna and plant life on the island, all of which evolved in relative isolation over the past 86 million years. There are daily walks, night hikes and lectures and discussions about Madagascar's biodiversity, which includes some 75% of the world's known chameleon species. There is also time to enjoy local food, art and Malagasy culture.\par \par \uc2 Cost: From $11,650\par \par \uc2 Cuba revealed\par \par \uc2 The University of Utah\uc2 's continuing-education program, Go Learn, sends travelers to out-of-the-way places such as Bhutan but also for deep dives in more popular destinations, such as studying archaeology in Mexico and theater in London. Utah Professor Emeritus of Economics Al Campbell leads an annual trip to Cuba, preceded by two \uc2 Zoom\uc2  classes on that nation's politics and economy. Once in-country, the travelers join discussions about Cuba's culture and economy with local professors and entrepreneurs. There are also visits to jazz clubs, the beach, Old Havana, farms and Unesco biosphere reserves. None of the speakers is state-sponsored or works for the Cuban government, Campbell says. The next trip is in early 2026 and is sold out. Sign-ups are typically eight months before departure.\par \par \uc2 Cost: From $6,000\par \par \uc2 Central Asia's five 'Stans\par \par \uc2 Study trips organized by Harvard Alumni Travels are open to the general public. This 18-day adventure is led by Timothy J. Colton, chair of the Department of Government at Harvard, who specializes in Russian and post-Soviet government and politics. The journey begins in Bishkek, capital of Kyrgyzstan. Other ancient Silk Road centers of intellectual exchange and commerce follow: Almaty, Kazakhstan; Dushanbe, Tajikistan; Tashkent and Samarkand in Uzbekistan. The route also crosses stunning mountains and steppes before ending in Ashgabat, Turkmenistan.\par \par \uc2 Cost: From $11,595\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Brigid Mander is a writer in Wyoming. She can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250823el8n00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
