\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: More Companies to Slow Hiring in Second Half\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ray A. Smith \par \uc2 261 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 One in five U.S. employers surveyed by the research group the Conference Board plans to slow hiring in the second half of 2025, nearly twice the percentage a year ago.\par \par \uc2 This marks the second year in a row that many polled chief human-resource officers planned on fewer new hires. Hiring optimism has steadily declined since the second quarter of 2023, according to past surveys by the Conference Board, which has more than 1,000 corporations as members.\par \par \uc2 Novo Nordisk\uc2 , the company behind the blockbuster drugs Ozempic and Wegovy, said Wednesday it would pause hiring in noncritical areas. \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2 , which has been on a hiring blitz for AI workers, is pausing that effort.\par \par \uc2 The staffing company \uc2 GEE Group\uc2  cited hiring freezes as a headwind to its business, and \uc2 AMN Healthcare\uc2  said hiring freezes at hospitals were slowing its physician-staffing business, according to the research firm \uc2 AlphaSense\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hiring managers are "taking a more thoughtful, steady approach as economic and policy uncertainty lingers," said Diana Scott, leader of the Conference Board's U.S. human-capital center. Changes this year that have made business leaders cautious include tariffs and workplace raids.\par \par \uc2 It now takes the average worker 24 weeks to find a job after losing one, nearly a month longer than a year ago, federal data shows. The July jobs report showed hiring slowing sharply.\par \par \uc2 Some CEOs have been bragging to investors about their shrinking workforces and plans to be more productive with fewer employees.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250822el8m0001r\par }\page {\par\fs2