\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 Big Tech Is Eating Itself in Talent War --- Companies' scramble for AI hires uses unorthodox methods that imperil Silicon Valley's startup culture\b0\par\par\uc2 By Asa Fitch \par \uc2 890 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 Big Tech's insatiable thirst for AI talent is threatening to kill its golden goose.\par \par \uc2 Tech companies are paying AI researchers billions of dollars and using unorthodox tactics to grab the brightest minds. Their moves might help them near-term in the battle for AI supremacy, but they could also stifle a Silicon Valley innovation engine they badly need.\par \par \uc2 Alongside job offers worth as much as $1 billion, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2 -parent \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  have all indulged in some version of what has come to be called the "reverse acquihire": Instead of acquiring startups, they have hired away founders and top AI researchers -- or licensed startups' technology -- and left the husk of the businesses to either find new missions or be acquired by someone else.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  did it with \uc2 Inflection AI\uc2  last year, bringing in its Chief Executive \uc2 Mustafa Suleyman\uc2  to manage its Copilot AI business, and paid a $650 million licensing fee to the company. \uc2 Meta\uc2  did it in June with AI data-labeling specialist \uc2 Scale AI\uc2 , proffering a $14.8 billion investment in the company in exchange for CEO \uc2 Alexandr Wang\uc2  and a team of Scale employees.\par \par \uc2 Such moves suit the needs of the top tech companies in the current moment. The hires are pulled off quickly during an AI race that the companies see as a once-in-a-generation opportunity. They are relatively uncomplicated, with companies getting the talent and technology they want without the hassle of a post-acquisition integration effort. Perhaps most important, they don't need regulatory approval in an era when all of the companies are under some form of antitrust scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 There are incentives on the startup side, too. Poached researchers are in some cases getting pro-athlete-level salaries. Venture-capital funders largely aren't getting big returns in these deals, industry executives say, but they aren't totally losing their shirts.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that the moves challenge Silicon Valley's cultural foundations.\par \par \uc2 Silicon Valley's basic bargain has always been rooted in taking enormous risk in the hope for an equally enormous reward. Most startups fail, but those that succeed can be wildly successful, generating hundredfold or higher returns for their venture-capital backers and making employees -- many lured with the promise of equity -- rich.\par \par \uc2 It is an especially risky business for rank-and-file employees of venture-backed startups who are tied to the success of one company at a time, not a diversified portfolio of them.\par \par \uc2 But for many employees of startups hollowed out in reverse acquihires or passed over in Big Tech hiring sprees, the rewards haven't been very handsome.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Google\uc2  gutted a startup called Windsurf in a $2.4 billion deal in July, some of the remaining staff cried in the startup's office. The remainder of the company was quickly snapped up by another AI startup, but those employees almost certainly didn't get the kind of payday they were expecting. Before the \uc2 Google\uc2  deal, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  had been in talks to buy Windsurf for $3 billion in what would have been a standard-issue Silicon Valley acquisition.\par \par \uc2 A bunch of tech employees losing out on a payday might not seem all that significant. But Silicon Valley's innovation machine only works if it has an army of people who aren't founders or leading researchers moving its gears, the employees who handle sales, marketing, human resources or are part of large engineering teams. That's who's getting the short end of the stick.\par \par \uc2 "There are a ton of employees who are bought into this system, and the history and the tradition is you come out here, you try to make something of value and if it works out, everybody wins," said Jon Sakoda, the founding partner of Decibel, a venture-capital firm. "If you thought you had a share of a company and you actually didn't have a share of a company, there's a loss of trust."\par \par \uc2 If the reverse acquihire trend persists, there is a reasonable chance many people who would have been bold enough to join a risky startup will give more weight to their other options. They might instead go directly to big tech companies, in what might be a safer route for themselves but one that makes the pool of available startup talent shallower.\par \par \uc2 Big tech's hiring tactics could ultimately prove problematic not only for venture capitalists and startups but for the titans themselves. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  collectively have acquired more than 100 companies outright since 2020, and have invested in hundreds more, according to Dealogic figures.\par \par \uc2 Android was hardly a household name when \uc2 Google\uc2  paid $50 million for it back in 2005, but it has become the centerpiece of the company's mobile-phone strategy. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's acquisition of Annapurna Labs for $350 million in 2015 laid the foundation for its wide-ranging custom-chip effort.\par \par \uc2 It is clear enough why Big Tech is using its current playbook in the AI craze. But every time it does so, it erodes the very startup culture that has made Silicon Valley an unparalleled source of tech innovation.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich334\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichg