\par\par \b \uc2 Explosion Disrupts Nippon's Plan --- Crews work to restore operations at \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  after deadly blast\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita and Kris Maher \par \uc2 842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  promised to spend $14 billion to keep \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's aging plants humming for decades. A deadly blast at a mill near Pittsburgh will test the Japanese steelmaker's resolve through a costly crisis.\par \par \uc2 Crews are continuing to clean up and restore operations at the Clairton, Pa., plant where an explosion Monday killed two workers and injured 10 others. \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  officials said the blast reduced the company's ability to produce coke -- a key steelmaking input -- at its Mon Valley Works complex.\par \par \uc2 Jim Borkowski, an employee with 21 years of service, was among those transporting injured workers from the wreckage. "It was chaos; we ran to help whoever and do whatever we could," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mon Valley was a focal point of \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's negotiations with President Trump. Two months ago, he approved the purchase of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  after the Tokyo-based company significantly increased its investment commitments.\par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  pledged $2.2 billion for upgrades at Mon Valley by 2028, projects that would maintain steelmaking at the mill for years. The to-do list didn't include major repairs to Clairton's coke ovens. It will likely cost the company millions of dollars to return the site to normal operations.\par \par \uc2 The Clairton coking plant -- the nation's largest -- has dealt with operational concerns in the past. An explosion at the plant in 2010 injured 20 workers. A catastrophic fire on Christmas Eve 2018 knocked out the plant's emissions-control system for months afterward.\par \par \uc2 In February, two employees were sent to the hospital when gases ignited at a coke plant battery, which Allegheny County officials said caused an audible boom and surge in airborne pollutants.\par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  said work on the planned replacement of the rolling line to make sheet steel would continue, but added, "Our primary focus remains on our employees at our Clairton plant."\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Dave Burritt\uc2  said Tuesday that he spoke with \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  executives following the accident and said they are committed to cooperating with government investigators to determine a cause for the explosion.\par \par \uc2 "We will take every step necessary to keep our people safe," Burritt said during a briefing with reporters. "We wouldn't have done the deal with \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  if we weren't absolutely sure that we would have an enduring future."\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  deferred some maintenance and upgrade projects for years in response to a long stretch of financial losses before 2020.\par \par \uc2 In the months before the deal closed, \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  Vice Chairman \uc2 Takahiro Mori\uc2  visited the Mon Valley complex several times to confirm the company's interest in preserving American jobs and investing in the mill. The steelmaker later dispatched teams of engineers and other employees from Japan to begin work on the plant upgrades.\par \par \uc2 The cause of the explosion remains under investigation. Just before the blast, workers were preparing to do routine maintenance on the batteries of ovens that bake coal into coke.\par \par \uc2 Coke is essential for producing molten iron at \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's blast furnaces in Pennsylvania and Indiana. As part of the coking process, flammable compounds, such as methane, and other impurities are extracted from the coal under heat and pressure, leaving a carbon product behind.\par \par \uc2 The Clairton plant can produce 3.6 million tons of coke across its six batteries of ovens. The explosion knocked out two batteries, and two more near the blast area were temporarily taken down for repairs.\par \par \uc2 Emissions from coke plants have been a target for environmental groups for years, especially in Pittsburgh, which has been a hub of steel production for over a century.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  last year settled a lawsuit brought by environmental groups over emissions that stemmed from the 2018 Clairton plant fire. The settlement with the groups and the county included $19.5 million in coke-oven upgrades and $17.5 million in other plant improvements.\par \par \uc2 The company has said it regretted the incidents and believed the settlement would benefit communities near the mill.\par \par \uc2 Experts in the litigation provided descriptions of the Clairton plant's condition.\par \par \uc2 In court filings, an engineering expert for environmental groups described plant inspections as "sporadic" and said that repairs were delayed by budget constraints. The expert cited an internal company email that mentioned examples of corrosion that included water-supply lines that were rotted "Coke-can thin."\par \par \uc2 A county health department official described the plant in a 2020 deposition as "one of the most decrepit facilities I've ever seen in my nearly 30 years of work."\par \par \uc2 Borkowski, 40, who works as a union-safety representative, said he thinks the company stays on top of maintenance "for the most part," but acknowledged that the company needs promised investments from \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's an aging plant," said Borkowski, a third-generation steelworker. "I just hope they stick to their word, and I hope they invest the money on the Mon Valley."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250815el8f0001l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
