\par\par \b \uc2 Walmart Perk to Ease Workers' Grocery Bills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Nassauer \par \uc2 534 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's newest employee benefit takes aim at a growing cost for many of its workers: their grocery bills.\par \par \uc2 The country's largest private employer said that, effective immediately, it was extending its 10% employee discount to include nearly all grocery purchases at its stores and online -- its latest effort to better recruit and retain workers.\par \par \uc2 The discount has long applied to fresh produce and general merchandise but excluded groceries such as milk, pasta, frozen pizza or meat, except during the November and December holiday-shopping season. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  workers have grumbled about the perk's limitations for years.\par \par \uc2 Walmart U.S. Chief Executive \uc2 John Furner\uc2  and a Miami-area store manager announced the news Wednesday on stage at a Houston gathering of store managers, prompting a standing ovation.\par \par \uc2 "All I can think is about my associates back home," the manager said, according to video viewed by The Wall Street Journal. "The associate pantry means everything to them because they don't know how they are going to be paying their next meal, and now this is going to help," he said, referencing informal backroom snacks in many stores.\par \par \uc2 Extending the discount to more goods year-round is "one of our most requested benefits," said \uc2 Walmart\uc2  Chief People Officer \uc2 Donna Morris\uc2  in a letter to staff Wednesday. \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's roughly 1.6 million U.S. workers receive the benefit after 90 days of employment. It excludes clearance items.\par \par \uc2 The change brings \uc2 Walmart\uc2  more in line with some competitors such as Target and \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2 . \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2 , owned by \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 , offers workers a 20% discount on most in-store purchases from the first day of employment. Target offers a 20% discount on fresh and frozen produce and some store brand items and a 10% discount on most other goods, which can be combined and are available on the first day of employment. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  provides a more limited perk to its U.S. warehouse and other hourly workers of a 10% discount, up to $100 saved each year.\par \par \uc2 Though the latest government data show that overall food prices held steady last month, they have risen sharply year over year for staples such as eggs, coffee and beef, according to the July consumer-price index report released this week. That adds to several years of persistent inflation.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's new perk is part of a string of additional benefits it has introduced in recent years, like higher wages for store managers and new potential bonuses for hourly workers. The move suggests the Bentonville, Ark.-based retailer continues to compete for hourly store workers as well as for business from inflation-weary shoppers. Morris said the discount aims to help "continue to make \uc2 Walmart\uc2  a great place to work."\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  declined to comment on what the estimated cost of expanding the benefit would be.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2  has been a retail winner, reporting years of quarterly sales growth. In recent years, shoppers have flocked to \uc2 Walmart\uc2  for low-price groceries to offset rising costs elsewhere and as it offers more premium products and fast-shipping services to attract high-income shoppers. The company reports its latest quarterly earnings next week.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich325\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4875
