\par\par \b \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  Begins 'Climb Back To Normalcy' After Shooting\b0\par\par\uc2 By Miriam Gottfried \par \uc2 833 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  hosts a weekly companywide call on Mondays at 8:30 a.m. that typically touches on business initiatives, deals or other happenings at the private-equity firm. This week's edition broke the mold.\par \par \uc2 Many workers were returning to the office a week after a gunman stormed the building that houses \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  's headquarters and shot and killed four people, including Wesley LePatner, one of the firm's top real-estate executives.\par \par \uc2 The call began with a montage of photos of LePatner, accompanied by Bruno Mars's "Count on Me," one of her favorite songs. \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Stephen Schwarzman\uc2  , President \uc2 Jonathan Gray\uc2  and Chief Administrative Officer Vik Sawhney shared stories of acts of bravery by various employees during the shooting and its aftermath.\par \par \uc2 There was the young employee who heard gunshots in the lobby and called to warn team members not to come down. A new hire who escorted a pregnant \uc2 National Football League\uc2  staffer to a secure location during the shooting. Employees on the 32nd floor who used furniture to build a huge barricade.\par \par \uc2 "We wanted to accomplish three things," Sawhney told The Wall Street Journal later Monday. "One was to celebrate and recognize Wesley. Two, was to acknowledge the trauma of our people and let them know how to get support, and the third was to start the hard climb back to normalcy."\par \par \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  has faced down tough times in the business world, but it hasn't experienced anything like the past week in its 40 years as a firm. Like many of its peers, it is known for a demanding culture, where a zeal to best rivals and boost profits comes with exacting standards and long hours. Those qualities have helped it win in a sometimes ruthless corner of Wall Street. The shooting has brought another side of \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  to the fore.\par \par \uc2 The firm on Friday told employees they could return this week to the New York office, a skyscraper at 345 Park Avenue, while also offering the option to continue to work remotely.\par \par \uc2 Many \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  staffers spent the past week finding ways to gather outside the office. Last Tuesday, the day after the July 28 shooting, \uc2 Nadeem Meghji\uc2  and Kathleen McCarthy Baldwin, global co-heads of real estate, hosted a 1,000-person \uc2 Zoom\uc2  call where people, some of whom hadn't slept the night before, shared memories of LePatner.\par \par \uc2 Later that day, around 200 of them gathered at Hudson River Park in the West Village to sit together and mourn, Meghji said.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 50 people from \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  's Tactical Opportunities group and a related fund convened Tuesday evening at a restaurant in the West Village. Smaller clusters of junior employees in the same pod and people working on the same project met up throughout the week for spontaneous dinners and drinks.\par \par \uc2 Employees who ventured in Monday were greeted by Schwarzman and Gray, who stationed themselves in the lobby just before 8 a.m. LePatner was among those shot in the lobby by the gunman, apparently angry at the NFL , another one of the building's tenants.\par \par \uc2 Some employees weren't ready to re-enter the building, but many did. A number of business leaders estimated that more than half of their teams were back.\par \par \uc2 People who showed up said they saw no evidence of the violence that had occurred there. Building owner Rudin Management and a cleanup crew hired by \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  worked around the clock to repair damage to the lobby and remove couches and other furniture on upper floors that had been piled up near doors to serve as barricades, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 "It was really difficult to enter the building because, even though it's been physically repaired, it's hard to move on from what happened in that space," Meghji said. Still, "the feeling of being together, even if we're not back to normal, has been very healing."\par \par \uc2 On the morning call, Schwarzman and Gray spoke about LePatner, detailing the firm's plans to donate to a charitable fund established by her family and to set up an annual award in her name. They spent the morning walking the floors, talking with employees. Schwarzman sat down with the real estate group for one of many big lunches \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  hosted.\par \par \uc2 The firm has been making counselors available via \uc2 Zoom\uc2  and in person in both individual and group settings. Sawhney said \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  will host a day of service for New York employees in the fall in honor of LePatner. It also plans donations in memory of the other three victims, a police officer, a security guard and a young Rudin employee.\par \par \uc2 The business of investing and dealmaking, largely paused last week, gradually began to resume. Investment-committee meetings, typically held on Mondays, were canceled this week. Leaders said they were initially trying to give priority to only the most important matters.\par \par \uc2 "Every day is like a step forward," Sawhney said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250806el860001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
