\par\par \b \uc2 Life Science: Trump Claims the Jobs Report Was Rigged. Was It?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allysia Finley \par \uc2 878 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Well, that was productive -- not. President Trump on Friday fired \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  Commissioner Erika McEntarfer after the July jobs report showed that hiring stalled this spring amid his tariff blitz and deportation crackdown. Shooting the messenger won't help him or the economy.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  estimates a mere 73,000 jobs were added last month, almost all in healthcare and social assistance. It also revised down gains for May and June by a combined 258,000, to a total of 33,000 new jobs, one of the biggest downward revisions in years.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump sniffs a deep-state conspiracy. "Today's Jobs Numbers were RIGGED in order to make the Republicans, and ME, look bad," the president huffed on Truth Social. Where's the evidence? There is none.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  commissioner has traditionally been a nonpartisan post, and the Senate confirmed Ms. McEntarfer 86-8 last year. Yet Mr. Trump asserted, again without evidence, that Ms. McEntarfer "faked the Jobs Numbers before the Election to try and boost Kamala's chances of Victory" and revised down the numbers after the election by 818,000. Also false.\par \par \uc2 The truth is that the jobs numbers have become more volatile in recent years because of declining business survey response rates. It's similar to the problem political pollsters face getting representative samples. And Mr. Trump's trade and immigration policies may be making monthly data less reliable.\par \par \uc2 It helps to understand how the \uc2 BLS\uc2  produces its monthly jobs report. The bureau surveys some 631,000 workplaces by a variety of media, including phone, web and even fax. Many businesses don't respond every month, but the \uc2 BLS\uc2  continues to collect data and revise its findings over the next two months.\par \par \uc2 The survey's overall response rate has declined to 43% from 60% before the pandemic, and small businesses are less likely than bigger ones to respond, especially in the first month. The jobs estimate can also be off in either direction by 136,000 in any given month because of statistical chance. Such variations tend to even out over several months.\par \par \uc2 The survey also doesn't fully account for business births and deaths. The \uc2 BLS\uc2  attempts to do so by using a model that incorporates other government data. When the economy is rapidly expanding, this model tends to underestimate new businesses relative to failures. But when the economy is slowing, the model typically does the opposite.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  adjusts its job numbers and models annually based on more-comprehensive records from state unemployment insurance agencies, but there's a lag. That's why the bureau last August revised its job estimates down by 818,000 over the 12 months that ended in March 2024. If Ms. McEntarfer were trying to help \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , why would she have done this before the election?\par \par \uc2 One plausible explanation for last year's big downward revision may be that the \uc2 BLS\uc2  model overestimated business formation and hiring by smaller establishments that didn't respond to its surveys. Small businesses were especially harmed by the Biden regulatory blitz and inflation. The same is true of Mr. Trump's tariffs and immigration raids.\par \par \uc2 It's also plausible that small businesses that responded late to the \uc2 BLS\uc2  surveys this spring hired fewer employees or laid off more of them than bigger establishments. Mom-and-pop restaurants might have lost immigrant workers and struggled to replace them. Small manufacturers with tight margins might have reduced jobs in response to tariff costs and uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Federation of Independent Business\uc2  survey last month notably showed plans to create jobs and increase compensation falling to the 2016 level of the slow-growing Obama economy. Most Americans give Mr. Trump low marks on the economy in contrast to his first term. The U.S. may not be in a recession, but Americans didn't vote for economic malaise.\par \par \uc2 While most employers aren't doing mass layoffs, hiring has slowed to a trickle. Nurses, doctors and construction workers can find jobs, but few others can. Unemployment has climbed most among teens and new college grads. Social Security claims spiked this spring, perhaps because older employees are taking employer buyouts and retiring.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump tacitly conceded that the economy is losing steam by lashing out Friday at Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  for being "TOO LATE" to cut interest rates. While the president has resisted his itch to fire Mr. Powell, firing Ms. McEntarfer is sure to undermine public and investor confidence in government data just the same.\par \par \uc2 What does Mr. Trump plan to do if next month's jobs numbers are meh or worse? Order the \uc2 BLS\uc2  to change them? Democrats are sure to accuse his administration of suppressing unfavorable economic data in any case. After Ms. McEntarfer's defenestration, the president's advisers may be even more reluctant to bear bad economic news lest he say off with their heads too.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump does himself and his party no favors by waving away economic reality. Democrats spent the Biden presidency playing down inflation, and look where it got them.\par \par \uc2 (See related letters: "Letters to the Editor: The Defenestration of the BLS Commissioner" -- WSJ Aug. 6, 2025)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250804el8400009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
