\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Reasons Why Managers Are So Miserable at Work\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ray A. Smith \par \uc2 1,049 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees around the world are feeling less happy about work than they have in years, with managers reporting the sharpest declines.\par \par \uc2 The percentage of employees who say they feel engaged -- that is, involved and enthusiastic about their work and workplace -- fell to 21% last year from 23% the year before, according to Gallup's annual State of the Global Workplace report. It was only the second time global employee engagement has declined in the 15 years Gallup has tracked it. The last time was in 2020, during the unease of Covid-19 lockdowns, Gallup says.\par \par \uc2 The decline was particularly pronounced among managers. Only 27% reported feeling engaged at work in 2024, a 3-point drop from the year before, with younger managers and female managers experiencing big declines. (Gallup started tracking manager engagement globally in 2022.) So far this year, manager engagement is little changed, the researchers say.\par \par \uc2 Managers have been grappling with a series of workplace disruptions in recent years, including a renewed focus by companies on efficiency, restructurings that have increased managerial workloads and hybrid schedules that can make it harder to keep track of employees.\par \par \uc2 By 2024, managers oversaw nearly three times as many people as they did in 2017, according to research and advisory firm \uc2 Gartner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's "very rare" to see drops of more than 2 percentage points in annual employee or manager engagement, says Jim Harter, chief scientist, workplace, at Gallup since 2003.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal spoke with Harter about why managers are so unhappy -- and what that means for business.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Why are managers more disengaged now?\par \par \uc2 JIM HARTER: They already had a lot on their plates, including meeting senior leadership's expectations, communicating changes, administrative things like timesheets, motivating high performers, developing star performers and keeping people. And now suddenly you've got a workplace where there are all these disruptions happening.\par \par \uc2 You've got postpandemic job reshuffling, a hiring boom and then bust, restructuring of teams, changing and sometimes shrinking budgets. The advent of AI and digital transformation. Flexible work expectations, which put more burden on managers to keep track of people. So the combination of an already high-demand job combined with these recent changes is a big reason.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: What stood out about the 3-percentage-point decline in manager engagement in 2024?\par \par \uc2 HARTER: This is very rare. When tracking global employee engagement for the full workforce, we have never seen a 3-point drop. Even a 2-point drop for the population as a whole has only occurred twice -- this year and in 2020.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Is not being in the office as much as previous generations playing a role in that?\par \par \uc2 HARTER: Globally, only 37% of young managers have received any training compared with 44% for managers overall.\par \par \uc2 They also are becoming more detached from their employer and regular mentorship due to being hybrid and having to keep track of where the people they manage are.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Female managers' engagement fell 7 percentage points. What accounts for that?\par \par \uc2 HARTER: On average across the globe, women take on more of the caregiving responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 So the return-to-office push and being pulled away from flexible work arrangements is causing some of the lack of engagement.\par \par \uc2 It's also about becoming more detached from regular mentorship. The recent years' disruptions have probably compromised their opportunities to get developmental support to become better managers.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: What are companies getting wrong when it comes to training managers?\par \par \uc2 HARTER: The training they provide is probably administrative-type stuff like timesheets and performance management. Managers note, more than anything else, that they've just had fewer opportunities for development than in the past.\par \par \uc2 According to our study, only 44% of managers ever had training to be a manager.\par \par \uc2 If you want to improve engagement of managers, training needs to focus on helping them form habits such as keeping in regular touch with the people they manage and asking them questions like, "What kind of challenges are you facing in your job?"\par \par \uc2 We did 17 studies of this kind of training during and after the pandemic with almost 15,000 participants and found the managers who received it were anywhere from 10% to 22% more highly engaged in their work.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Why do those kinds of habits improve manager engagement?\par \par \uc2 HARTER: Being in regular communication with employees helps managers get ahead of issues, learn what is happening front-line in the moment and give recognition for great work. That makes their jobs clearer and more inspiring.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Gallup once found that companies often fail to choose managerial candidates with the right talents for the job. Is that still a problem?\par \par \uc2 HARTER: When we ask managers how they got their job, the top two reasons they give consistently are: I was a high performer as an individual contributor, or I had enough length of service to be promoted. On the surface, those seem like equitable reasons to move someone into management. But they don't really say anything about someone's ability to manage people effectively. Let's say an employee is a really good salesperson and gets promoted to manager. Once they are in that role they may become frustrated because managing people is a very different deal.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Why should anyone care that managers are feeling so disengaged?\par \par \uc2 HARTER: When managers are engaged, the teams they lead tend to be more engaged. And engaged teams achieve much higher levels of performance. Their productivity goes up.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: What is the one thing managers can do that would make the biggest difference in their own engagement?\par \par \uc2 HARTER: I would say the cadence with which they have meaningful conversations with the people that they manage. Some managers go into the job thinking that if they talk to the people they manage once a quarter or once every six months, that they've checked the box. But things are changing very quickly in organizations.\par \par \uc2 Managers need to be in touch with their people on a regular basis, involving them rather than just delegating to them, and noticing what people are doing right.\par \par \uc2 What engages a manager is "here's the kind of impact my job can have on other people's lives."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250804el840000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
