\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Labor Statistic Chief Fired by Trump Viewed as Forthcoming, Nonpartisan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Grossman \par \uc2 816 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 About a year into her tenure as the head of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , Erika McEntarfer stood before the Atlanta Economics Club and laid out what she saw as two obstacles to her job producing the government's economic data: the cost of doing that work was rising, and the number of people responding to surveys was declining.\par \par \uc2 The bureau, she said, would aim to modernize and keep going. "If we succeed, the U.S. can continue to have this rich set of economic data," she told the group.\par \par \uc2 One challenge she didn't see coming: the ire of the president, who fired her on Friday after the bureau posted a surprisingly dismal jobs report. President Trump accused her agency of having rigged the numbers to make him look bad. Statisticians and economists leapt to her defense and worried that Trump's move would undermine the integrity of U.S. economic data.\par \par \uc2 The episode has made McEntarfer, a 52-year-old labor economist who favors thick-framed glasses and a well-placed "Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" reference, an unlikely central character in a political drama over the direction of the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 McEntarfer spent most of her career at the Census Bureau, where she led a program to produce economic statistics with finer-grained detail on people and places. In 2022, she joined former President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2 . Biden then nominated her to lead the \uc2 BLS\uc2 , and she won confirmation early last year in an 86-8 vote in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Her supporters point out that as a career federal employee, she worked with administrations of both parties in various capacities. Colleagues describe McEntarfer as a levelheaded, nonpartisan analyst and administrator.\par \par \uc2 "She has an impeccable record," The Friends of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , an independent organization that advocates for transparent government statistics, wrote in a public statement after she was fired.\par \par \uc2 McEntarfer didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 During Trump's first administration, his daughter \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  led a White House initiative to bolster workforce training which included McEntarfer, then at Census, said William Beach, a first-term Trump appointee who led the \uc2 BLS\uc2  immediately before McEntarfer. Beach, who was also involved in the effort, said McEntarfer was an important member of a team working on statistics for the project, which ended when Trump left office. \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  couldn't be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 BLS\uc2  commissioner, McEntarfer oversaw more than 2,000 federal workers, split between a headquarters near Washington, D.C., and regional offices around the country.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  is a part of the Labor Department, but operates at arm's length from what \uc2 BLS\uc2  staffers call "main Labor," to insulate data integrity from political interference. As commissioner, McEntarfer would have been one of the few \uc2 BLS\uc2  staff to have regular interactions with the broader Trump administration, former officials said.\par \par \uc2 Though McEntarfer earned the respect of her peers as \uc2 BLS\uc2  commissioner, she also presided over a number of embarrassing stumbles that raised questions about the agency's communications.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported that in February of last year, a small group of Wall Street analysts and economists received an email from a \uc2 BLS\uc2  economist that may have given professionals privileged insights into how certain housing inflation statistics were calculated. The \uc2 BLS\uc2  said afterward that it tries to be responsive to users' data questions and said it would review its policies.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  has faced other strains. The Wall Street Journal reported this year that the agency has increasingly relied on guesswork to calculate some of its biggest data releases because of staff shortages caused by a federal hiring freeze. Democratic senators recently pressed the \uc2 BLS\uc2  for more information.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of workers staff the labor-intensive surveys needed to calculate the nation's inflation rate and unemployment rate. At headquarters, \uc2 BLS\uc2  economists translate the numbers flowing in from around the country into monthly reports that policymakers, business leaders and investors rely on to make decisions.\par \par \uc2 "We face real challenges at the federal level. . .which endanger much of the official data that I've been talking about today," McEntarfer said at her Atlanta appearance in January.\par \par \uc2 Still, she said, decades of researchers' attempts to improve upon the official statistics with private-sector substitutes have only proven that the old-fashioned stats are still unbeatable. "Even now, with all the data we are watching in our digital economy, the official economic indicators remain fundamental," McEntarfer said.\par \par \uc2 McEntarfer put a frank, no-nonsense face on the \uc2 BLS\uc2 , said Robert Santos, who as former director of the Census Bureau collaborated with her during her time as \uc2 BLS\uc2  commissioner. "She's very straight with you, forthcoming," Santos said. She "tells you when she likes things, tells you when she doesn't."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich444\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6660
