\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 What to Do in Active-Shooter Situations --- More workplaces are having to prepare for the rare, nightmare scenario -- how should workers get training or plan?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter \par \uc2 1,041 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's a rare, nightmare scenario yet one that more people have confronted in recent years: A shooter shows up at your workplace -- as one did at a Park Avenue office tower in New York City -- forcing quick decisions.\par \par \uc2 Shane Tamura, 27 years old, used an AR-15-style rifle to kill an \uc2 NYPD\uc2  police officer in the lobby, as well as a security guard, an executive of financial firm Blackstone and a real-estate firm employee before shooting himself in the chest last month, police said.\par \par \uc2 For years, the most commonly advised approach to active shooter situations has boiled down to three words: run, hide, fight. But many security experts say that thinking has evolved and that additional tactics, such as building barricades, breaking through windows and creating distractions, can be critical to survival.\par \par \uc2 Here's what the latest training in active-shooter situations says are best practices:\par \par \uc2 What does 'run, hide, fight' mean?\par \par \uc2 Training courses use a variety of names to describe the basic active-shooter protocol, but the tactics are largely the same. The priority in "run, hide, fight" is the "run," security specialists say. "You want to escape if you can," said Jim Hayes, a veteran of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , who now advises companies in his role as a vice president at the security firm \uc2 Guidepost Solutions\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If fleeing isn't an option, try to find a hiding spot in an out-of-the-way room. Some companies also teach "ABC," an acronym that instructs people to avoid the threat, barricade themselves and, as a last resort, confront an assailant. Other training programs prefer "ALICE," which stands for alert, lock down, inform, counter and evacuate.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is the same: "Running or getting out gives you the best chance of success," Hayes said. "Then, if you can't do that, what's the next best option?"\par \par \uc2 What are the best ways to hide?\par \par \uc2 In the Park Avenue shooting, workers hid in bathrooms, conference rooms and other areas, using blankets to block windows. Some created barricades of office sofas and desks in front of doors. That can delay a shooter from getting to a hiding spot before law-enforcement officers arrive, security specialists say.\par \par \uc2 Use the heaviest furniture or equipment available to block entrances, such as desks, filing cabinets and tables. Try to hide along the wall closest to the exit but out of sight -- that provides the best chances for ambushing the shooter or escaping if the shooter enters or walks past. Many shooters know they have limited time, so if an area is hard to reach, they might move on, security specialists say. "Generally, these people are not going to spend a lot of time trying to get into a locked room or a barricaded room," Hayes said.\par \par \uc2 Can you plan in advance?\par \par \uc2 Yes, said Dale Buckner, chief executive of the security firm \uc2 Global Guardian\uc2 . Creating a "what if?" escape plan can save seconds, even minutes in an emergency.\par \par \uc2 Take the "run."\par \par \uc2 Do you know where the various exits and access points are in your workplace? If elevators are shut off, would you know how to get downstairs? If a colleague is in a wheelchair, or is older and might need assistance, who is ready to help them?\par \par \uc2 "Those little things matter," Buckner said.\par \par \uc2 Map out your office to determine whether certain rooms are more secure from gunfire, should you need to hide. That could be a conference room without windows or a storage closet with cinder-block walls.\par \par \uc2 Should I pull the fire alarm?\par \par \uc2 Federal law-enforcement officials generally advise against that. People are conditioned to immediately evacuate a building when they hear the alarm, potentially putting them in harm's way.\par \par \uc2 A fire alarm also risks disabling elevators -- which could be needed to help injured people -- or unlocking certain doors, increasing a shooter's ability to move throughout a building.\par \par \uc2 When to hide or fight?\par \par \uc2 Fighting is seen as a last resort. But when a gunman has found your hiding place and is pointing a weapon in your direction, it is time to switch from hiding to confronting. Fighting can mean throwing something at a gunman -- a fire extinguisher, a chair, even a handful of pens -- to create a distraction, improvising a weapon or creating chaos in the room by yelling and running around. Law-enforcement officials recommend that, when in fight mode, it is best to act aggressively.\par \par \uc2 How about communication?\par \par \uc2 Silence all electronic devices, including ringers and vibrate functions.\par \par \uc2 If possible, text or send direct messages to law enforcement over social media to share your location. Try to report any active-shooter emergency via 911 first; use social media only as a backup, not as a replacement for contacting emergency services directly.\par \par \uc2 How can I get training?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\uc2  maintains resources for active-shooter preparedness, with advice for workers and employers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  posts videos on its website and other tips for knowing how to respond.\par \par \uc2 Some local police departments, universities, schools and government agencies also conduct active-shooter training, even if your company doesn't.\par \par \uc2 What should training include?\par \par \uc2 Besides running through "run, hide, fight" protocols, security specialists say training courses should emphasize that employees can play a role in spotting potential threats. Take any unsolicited comments about violence, acts of revenge or firearms seriously and report any threats of violence to supervisors or human resources.\par \par \uc2 "Colleagues are the ones who can identify a significant change," said \uc2 Brian Stephens\uc2  of \uc2 Teneo\uc2 , who advises companies about security matters and is a former chief security officer at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many security experts also advise "stop the bleed" training, a national program to teach bystanders how to treat gunshot and other life-threatening wounds before first responders arrive.\par \par \uc2 An analysis of the 2016 Pulse nightclub shooting in Orlando found that 16 people died because quick care wasn't provided to survivable wounds.\par \par \uc2 Some students have survived school shootings because teachers had bleeding-control training and knew how to properly apply gauze and tourniquets.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250804el840000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
