\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Democrats Against a Higher Minimum Wage\b0\par\par\uc2 477 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 File this under the category of progressives mugged by reality: On Monday the District of Columbia's City Council voted to slow a rising minimum wage for tipped workers, which has ravaged restaurants. To adapt an old adage for a young Washington clientele, there's no such thing as a free brunch.\par \par \uc2 Tipped workers, including restaurant servers, are often paid at a special lower hourly wage, but then they earn much higher take-home pay. D.C. voters passed a ballot initiative in 2022 to raise the tipped minimum wage (then $5.35) to match the city's ordinary wage floor (now $17.95) by 2027. At this point, the tipped wage mandate has already nearly doubled to $10.\par \par \uc2 As restaurants with thin margins struggled to eat the payroll increases, Democratic Mayor Muriel Bowser called to repeal the change and revert the tipped wage to $5.95. The City Council didn't go that far, but it did cap the final increase at 75% of the full minimum, while delaying that endpoint until 2034. This summer's jump to $12 is canceled.\par \par \uc2 It's welcome relief. More than 50 D.C. restaurants have shut down this year, according to the local trade association, which puts the city on pace to break its record for closings -- which was set last year. Before the city delayed this summer's increase, two in five full-service, casual restaurants warned that they were likely to close by the end of 2025, according to an industry survey.\par \par \uc2 "There was an activist who told me, 'To get to the other side, sacrifices have to be made,'" an owner of Brookland's Finest Bar and Kitchen, which shut after 11 years, told a local Fox station. "Well, I would have preferred it not be my staff that got sacrificed." Aside from the closures, patrons have also noticed higher menu prices and new "service charges."\par \par \uc2 One irony is that for many servers the supposed pay raise has been a pay cut in disguise. In one survey cited by the restaurant association, 79% of tipped workers said they were getting less money in tips, and 56% were working fewer hours. Overall earnings for a median tipped worker in D.C. have dropped 5% since 2023, or about $1,800 a year, according to the Employment Policies Institute.\par \par \uc2 D.C.'s epiphany is the latest loss for One Fair Wage, an activist group that has pushed to end separate minimum wages for tipped workers. Massachusetts voters rejected a ballot proposal in November, and Michigan lawmakers rolled back a tipped-wage phaseout in February.\par \par \uc2 But an effort succeeded in Chicago, which started eliminating its tip credit last summer. Restaurant owners say that change is already resulting in job losses. How many times must Democratic cities and states relearn the laws of economics?\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250802el8200023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
