\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- The Leaner, Meaner Chevron --- CEO \uc2 Mike Wirth\uc2  is overhauling the oil giant's 'nice' corporate culture, getting tougher not just on his employees but also on rivals and the politicians standing in his way\b0\par\par\uc2 By Collin Eaton \par \uc2 1,903 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Chief Executive \uc2 Mike Wirth\uc2  had a stern message for \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's 40,000 employees in February: Stop being so nice to each other.\par \par \uc2 Leaders need to be more decisive, take accountability for failures and have uncomfortable conversations about poor performance, Wirth and his lieutenants said during a virtual companywide meeting. A change in attitude could keep \uc2 Chevron\uc2  from falling behind its competitors, they said, according to people who attended the meeting.\par \par \uc2 The effort to overhaul \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's corporate culture emanated from a report the company commissioned from consulting firm McKinsey that found employees were frustrated. The company wasn't innovative, and it lacked a clear strategy, they said.\par \par \uc2 "We are 'nice' even when desired results or behaviors are not achieved," according to McKinsey's summary of employee feedback, which was reviewed by The Wall Street Journal. "We emphasize collaboration and inclusion to build strong teams . . . but we do not have direct, uncomfortable conversations needed to deliver results."\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2  executives turned to McKinsey last year as its shares lagged behind rival \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2 's amid headwinds in key parts of its oil business. Its major oil project in Kazakhstan had hit costly snags. As oil prices moderated, drilling slowed in the Permian Basin, the largest U.S. oil field. And, most notably, its $53 billion acquisition of Hess was stalled after a shocking move by \uc2 Exxon\uc2  to try to intervene in the deal.\par \par \uc2 Now, the $310 billion oil giant, the second largest in the U.S. behind \uc2 Exxon\uc2 , is getting tougher not just on its employees but also on rivals, political detractors and anyone else it encounters.\par \par \uc2 After outlining a sweeping reorganization of its business units on the February call, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  announced plans to cut roughly 8,000 jobs, or up to 20% of its workforce, through 2026, part of a plan to save up to $3 billion. And two weeks ago, the Hess deal finally closed.\par \par \uc2 "I'm incredibly proud of \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's culture and wouldn't trade it for anybody else's," Wirth said in a statement to The Wall Street Journal this past week. "We're working to further strengthen our culture with an even sharper focus on performance by executing faster and more efficiently, simplifying our organization, and delivering targeted innovation."\par \par \uc2 For over a century, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  was California's quintessential oil company, fostering a more genteel and mellow corporate environment than that of its bigger rival, the more rigid and often litigious Exxon of Texas. \uc2 Chevron\uc2  had long been considered by industry veterans the more comfortable, collaborative place to work. It still aims to adhere to principles that boil down to treating people fairly, but with a sharper focus on delivery.\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's corporate history traces back to 1876, when wildcatters drilled California's first successful oil well in the Santa Susana Mountains, north of Los Angeles. The subsequent oil boom drove the economic development of California. \uc2 Chevron\uc2  eventually became the state's largest employer, with its logo on every other gas station, its name in opera playbills and museum brochures, and its leaders enjoying easy access to the state's governors and lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 In the 1990s, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  embraced a movement that was popular in California and became the first major oil company to include sexual orientation in its equal-employment-opportunity policies. It extended benefits to same-sex couples, despite opposition in other places where it operated like Texas and Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Wirth, 64, has been an engineer and \uc2 Chevron\uc2  employee for over 40 years and rose to chairman and CEO in 2018. At the time, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  was facing cost overruns on large projects. Wirth was seen as a cost-conscious executive, having led the company's refining and chemicals business -- in which margins are critical -- for almost a decade.\par \par \uc2 The deal for Hess, first announced in October 2023, was seen by Wall Street as critical to securing \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's long-term oil reserves and profits while cementing Wirth's legacy as a transformational leader. Instead, \uc2 Exxon\uc2  stepped in and essentially said not so fast. It asserted a right of first refusal to bid for \uc2 Hess\uc2 's 30% stake in a generational oil discovery in Guyana -- a move that stunned \uc2 Chevron\uc2  executives and the oil community at large.\par \par \uc2 Exxon\uc2  has a 45% stake in the project in Guyana and serves as the operator of vessels pumping about 650,000 barrels of oil a day there. By 2027, daily production is expected to reach 1.2 million barrels, making it one of the most coveted projects in the world as many oil fields deplete and new exploration can take decades to pay off.\par \par \uc2 For nearly two years, \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's acquisition of Hess hung in limbo while the companies battled over the Guyana crown jewel.\par \par \uc2 Early on, analysts, bankers and investors predicted \uc2 Chevron\uc2  would negotiate some offering to \uc2 Exxon\uc2  -- perhaps a percentage of Hess's stake, for instance --- to avoid arbitration.\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2  held firm. It never restarted talks after \uc2 Exxon\uc2  filed for arbitration, even as it waited more than a year for the process to begin.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, investors were clamoring about \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's growth prospects without Hess and questioning whether it would need to land another megadeal if \uc2 Exxon\uc2  prevailed.\par \par \uc2 Losing Hess would have marked the second major transaction that Wirth failed to close as CEO. He backed away from a potential bidding war with \uc2 Occidental Petroleum\uc2  in 2019 over \uc2 Anadarko Petroleum\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2  ultimately prevailed in the arbitration dispute last month -- but it wasn't without some hard feelings.\par \par \uc2 "It has been a long process, and it didn't need to be," Wirth said in an interview July 18, the day \uc2 Chevron\uc2  closed the Hess acquisition. "It should have been resolved quicker. The outcome was never in doubt."\par \par \uc2 "After 2027, it was very murky if they had anything in the funnel," Jean Ann Salisbury, an analyst at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , said of \uc2 Chevron\uc2 . Now, with the Hess deal completed, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  is positioned to "close much of the gap" with \uc2 Exxon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To do that, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  is aiming to become leaner and meaner.\par \par \uc2 Exxon\uc2  has long been known as a merit-driven organization that doesn't shy away from a fight. With its army of lawyers and its extensive time in court -- most often because of lawsuits filed against it by cities and states over climate change -- it has been likened by some to a law firm that produces oil.\par \par \uc2 Exxon\uc2  suffered a rare stumble during the pandemic when the company booked a $20 billion loss for 2020. \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's market value briefly eclipsed \uc2 Exxon\uc2 's for the first time that October. Later, \uc2 Exxon\uc2  lost a bruising proxy fight to a little-known activist, prompting its executives to double down on protecting their turf. Last year, the company sued two sustainability investors for making climate-related shareholder proposals. The investment firms backed off, and at this year's annual meeting, \uc2 Exxon\uc2  encountered no shareholder proposals.\par \par \uc2 Exxon\uc2 's move to block the deal between \uc2 Chevron\uc2  and \uc2 Hess\uc2  was especially pugnacious. Analysts said \uc2 Exxon\uc2  had little to lose -- and was successful on some level by stalling \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's capture of a slice of Guyana's riches.\par \par \uc2 Wirth moved \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's corporate headquarters last year from California, where it had resided for more than 140 years. Relations had soured with the state government. In moving to Houston, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  joined \uc2 Exxon\uc2  and much of the rest of the industry.\par \par \uc2 Throughout its history, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  had "attracted a lot of people who were from California or went to college in California," which contributed to a more open-minded and neighborly attitude at the company, said \uc2 Edward Chow\uc2 , a \uc2 Chevron\uc2  executive for over 20 years until 1999. "Now, to me, it feels more like another oil company. The distinction between \uc2 Exxon\uc2  and \uc2 Chevron\uc2  has narrowed in terms of corporate culture."\par \par \uc2 By the time Wirth decamped for more fossil-friendly Texas from \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's campus in San Ramon, east of San Francisco, \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's relationship with the Golden State and Democratic Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  had broken down.\par \par \uc2 California acted more aggressively than any other state to steer consumers away from fossil fuels and claimed oil companies were price gouging. In response, \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's executives launched a public-relations campaign in California that blamed lawmakers for the state's high prices. The campaign included big signs with QR codes at \uc2 Chevron\uc2  gas stations that urged drivers to complain directly to lawmakers starting around 2022.\par \par \uc2 In November, Wirth said California's state government was putting bureaucrats in charge of key parts of the economy, a move that "hasn't worked in other socialist states."\par \par \uc2 In January, about three weeks after President Trump suggested the U.S. should rename the Gulf of Mexico the Gulf of America, Wirth told investors \uc2 Chevron\uc2  would begin calling the region the Gulf of America. "That's the position of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  now," he said.\par \par \uc2 Wirth has built strong ties with several officials in the Trump administration. The oil industry has enlisted the administration in fights against clean-car rules, drilling restraints and climate laws from New York to California. Meanwhile, the financial world has all but turned away from the environmental concerns that pervaded banks and big investment firms in the Biden years, easing pressure on oil producers. Executives are trying to use their political sway to push for the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to streamline permitting for pipelines and encourage fossil-fuel export deals.\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's lobbying efforts with President Trump and his cabinet secretaries paid off again last month, this time in South America.\par \par \uc2 In May, the Trump administration pulled \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's Biden-era license to pump oil in Venezuela to clamp down on socialist dictator \uc2 Nicolas Maduro\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2  has taken political risks to remain active in the South American country, which has one of the world's largest oil reserves, and has argued its presence keeps China and Russia out of the region.\par \par \uc2 A week ago, the Trump administration abruptly reversed course and said it would allow \uc2 Chevron\uc2  to continue pumping oil in Venezuela after a prisoner swap released the 10 remaining Americans who were detained by the government. On an earnings call Friday, Wirth told analysts that a limited amount of Venezuelan crude will start flowing to the U.S. this month, "consistent with U.S. sanctions policy."\par \par \uc2 Wirth has work to do to keep investors happy as oil companies struggle to attract shareholders. The S&P 500's energy sector has shrunk to a tiny percentage of the overall index, and many investors became skeptical of frackers' ability to make money during America's shale boom. Oil prices have slipped this year on fears that tariff fights could squeeze fuel demand and as big producers in the Middle East put more oil into circulation.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  executives said \uc2 Hess\uc2  will add billions of dollars to its cash flow. But there will be cost cuts. \uc2 Chevron\uc2  recently informed Texas regulators it plans to cut 575 jobs in Houston as part of the merger.\par \par \uc2 The aim is to enable workers "to get things done in a simpler way," \uc2 Mark Nelson\uc2 , vice chairman of \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , said on the call.\par \par \uc2 "I expect to see more than just cost reductions," he said. "I expect to see performance improvement across the system."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250802el820001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
